912
00:39:20,232 --> 00:39:22,234
{\an8}"Ruling, Jang C-store."

913
00:39:22,318 --> 00:39:24,028
{\an8}"Ordered to be placed
in juvenile detention"?

914
00:39:25,696 --> 00:39:27,698
C-store was the perpetrator?

915
00:39:28,991 --> 00:39:30,618
I see.

916
00:39:30,701 --> 00:39:32,828
C-store bullied Lee Gun-jun.

917
00:39:32,912 --> 00:39:34,663
This is a case of school violence.

918
00:39:35,164 --> 00:39:37,375
{\an8}WAS JANG C-STORE A SCHOOL BULLY?

919
00:39:37,458 --> 00:39:38,834
{\an8}Who would've thought, huh?

920
00:39:38,918 --> 00:39:40,086
{\an8}Wait, the trash can.

921
00:39:40,669 --> 00:39:42,046
{\an8}I forgot to check the trash can.

922
00:39:44,799 --> 00:39:45,716
What's this?

923
00:39:45,800 --> 00:39:46,634
SOMETHING BROKEN?

924
00:39:47,218 --> 00:39:48,302
A CCTV?

925
00:39:50,012 --> 00:39:51,013
{\an8}Why is it smashed?

926
00:39:51,097 --> 00:39:52,390
{\an8}A BROKEN SECURITY CAMERA

927
00:39:53,432 --> 00:39:54,350
What's this?

928
00:39:57,186 --> 00:39:58,187
{\an8}What did he do with this?

929
00:39:58,270 --> 00:39:59,230
{\an8}HAMMER

930
00:39:59,730 --> 00:40:01,023
{\an8}HANSIM POCHA

931
00:40:01,107 --> 00:40:02,400
{\an8}This is where everyone gathered.

932
00:40:02,483 --> 00:40:03,484
GANGDAM STATION YOUTH STREET MEETING

933
00:40:03,567 --> 00:40:06,695
{\an8}<i>A meeting was held at Hansim Pocha,</i>

934
00:40:06,779 --> 00:40:09,740
<i>and four of the five suspects
were present.</i>

935
00:40:09,824 --> 00:40:15,079
<i>I need to find out
if they were all there the entire time.</i>

936
00:40:15,621 --> 00:40:18,082
{\an8}So they had to choose between
a tiered payment according to annual sales

937
00:40:18,165 --> 00:40:20,334
{\an8}or an equal lump sum payment.

938
00:40:20,418 --> 00:40:22,920
The lump sum payment won more votes.

939
00:40:25,297 --> 00:40:27,216
REGISTRATION FORM FOR MEETING WITH
BUSINESS OWNERS AFFECTED BY COVID-19

940
00:40:27,299 --> 00:40:28,551
WED, AUGUST 12, 2020, 9:30 P.M.

941
00:40:28,634 --> 00:40:29,844
SUBMIT REGISTRATION FORM
TO THE CONSULTATION CENTER

942
00:40:29,927 --> 00:40:31,470
ONLY APPLICANTS ARE ELIGIBLE
TO RECEIVE THE SUPPORT FUND

943
00:40:31,554 --> 00:40:33,597
LIST OF ATTENDANTS
♪ PLEASE SIGN BELOW WHEN LEAVING

944
00:40:33,681 --> 00:40:35,975
Right. Kim Club attended.

945
00:40:36,058 --> 00:40:38,060
An Dancer attended as a proxy.

946
00:40:38,811 --> 00:40:40,938
Jang C-store filled in
for Hong Father-in-law.

947
00:40:41,021 --> 00:40:42,314
He didn't sign out.

948
00:40:42,398 --> 00:40:43,399
(EMPTY SIGNATURE FIELD)

949
00:40:45,609 --> 00:40:48,946
{\an8}"Useful information on COVID-19 damages
affecting small businesses."

950
00:40:50,489 --> 00:40:52,074
{\an8}"Joo, the tenant of store A

951
00:40:52,158 --> 00:40:55,119
{\an8}on Youth Street in Gangdam District,
had their business suspended

952
00:40:55,202 --> 00:40:56,495
{\an8}for operating illegally at night."

953
00:40:57,204 --> 00:40:58,372
{\an8}What's this?

954
00:40:58,998 --> 00:41:00,875
SENSIBILITY BAR

955
00:41:00,958 --> 00:41:02,626
Hold on a second.

956
00:41:03,169 --> 00:41:05,421
{\an8}All right, let's take a look.

957
00:41:05,504 --> 00:41:06,964
{\an8}THE BAR WHERE THE VICTIM,
LEE GUN-MI, WORKED

958
00:41:14,513 --> 00:41:16,390
{\an8}Sensibility Bar is closed.

959
00:41:17,016 --> 00:41:19,310
It received a two-week suspension penalty.

960
00:41:19,393 --> 00:41:20,436
"Due to a violation

961
00:41:20,519 --> 00:41:22,730
of the Infectious Disease Control
and Prevention Act."

962
00:41:22,813 --> 00:41:23,981
How do I get in?

963
00:41:24,857 --> 00:41:25,941
All the doors are locked.

964
00:41:26,942 --> 00:41:28,027
None of them opens.

965
00:41:29,361 --> 00:41:31,363
- Mr. Ha Gold Spoon, you must leave now.
- Right.

966
00:41:31,447 --> 00:41:32,698
I'm right behind you.

967
00:41:33,532 --> 00:41:36,368
Have you already forgotten
how kind I was to you last week?

968
00:41:36,452 --> 00:41:37,495
Give me five more minutes.

969
00:41:37,578 --> 00:41:38,662
No exceptions, sir.

970
00:41:38,746 --> 00:41:40,789
- No exceptions?
- You need to go.

971
00:41:40,873 --> 00:41:45,211
By the looks of it,
I should just escort you to jail.

972
00:41:45,294 --> 00:41:46,295
Me?

973
00:41:46,378 --> 00:41:47,338
Never mind.

974
00:41:47,421 --> 00:41:48,631
You'd better watch what you say.

975
00:41:48,714 --> 00:41:50,674
I was just messing around.

976
00:41:50,758 --> 00:41:52,718
People were way shadier than I expected.

977
00:41:52,801 --> 00:41:54,220
INITIAL EXAMINATION
OF CRIME SCENE CONCLUDED

978
00:41:55,888 --> 00:41:58,891
COMBINE THE CLUES

979
00:41:59,391 --> 00:42:01,227
{\an8}During this briefing,

980
00:42:01,310 --> 00:42:03,437
{\an8}I want you to ask all your questions

981
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:06,982
{\an8}and answer any questions directed at you
as best as you can.

982
00:42:07,608 --> 00:42:09,318
{\an8}We'll begin with Ms. Dancer.

983
00:42:10,361 --> 00:42:12,488
{\an8}Why don't you kick us off?

984
00:42:12,571 --> 00:42:13,989
{\an8}Sure thing.

985
00:42:14,573 --> 00:42:15,783
{\an8}FIRST BRIEFING: AN DANCER

986
00:42:15,866 --> 00:42:18,118
{\an8}I wonder where she's been poking around.

987
00:42:18,744 --> 00:42:20,204
{\an8}First things first,

988
00:42:21,080 --> 00:42:22,581
{\an8}I'd like to talk about the body.

989
00:42:22,665 --> 00:42:26,210
{\an8}The wound on her head stood out the most.

990
00:42:26,293 --> 00:42:29,129
{\an8}Whatever caused it left a deep gash.

991
00:42:29,213 --> 00:42:33,092
{\an8}That's why I believe the blow to the head

992
00:42:33,175 --> 00:42:34,927
{\an8}caused Ms. Gun-mi's death.

993
00:42:35,010 --> 00:42:37,346
{\an8}Why use such a formal address
to talk about your friend?

994
00:42:37,429 --> 00:42:40,307
{\an8}Because this is a formal setting, C-store.

995
00:42:41,809 --> 00:42:43,143
{\an8}Why can't you tell

996
00:42:43,227 --> 00:42:44,937
{\an8}when to speak and when not to?

997
00:42:45,563 --> 00:42:47,106
{\an8}Aren't you old enough to know better?

998
00:42:47,189 --> 00:42:51,068
{\an8}She said it was a formal setting,
yet she still called me C-store.

999
00:42:51,151 --> 00:42:52,444
{\an8}- You two are friends.
- What's that about?

1000
00:42:52,528 --> 00:42:53,696
{\an8}- Let's--
- You're still alive.

1001
00:42:53,779 --> 00:42:55,197
{\an8}Let's keep it professional.

1002
00:42:55,906 --> 00:42:57,575
All right, moving on.

1003
00:42:57,658 --> 00:43:00,744
The marks on her neck
suggested she had been strangled.

1004
00:43:00,828 --> 00:43:02,162
Agreed.

1005
00:43:02,246 --> 00:43:03,747
- Also--
- She was all banged up.

1006
00:43:03,831 --> 00:43:06,250
Exactly. Especially her arms.

1007
00:43:06,333 --> 00:43:09,837
{\an8}The cuts and bruises there could
only have been caused during a scuffle.

1008
00:43:09,920 --> 00:43:12,131
{\an8}But here's something suspicious.

1009
00:43:12,214 --> 00:43:13,799
{\an8}The soles of her shoes were too clean.

1010
00:43:13,882 --> 00:43:17,011
{\an8}Even accounting
for the rain washing them off,

1011
00:43:17,094 --> 00:43:18,804
{\an8}wouldn't there be
some sort of trace left behind

1012
00:43:18,887 --> 00:43:20,598
{\an8}after such a violent fight?

1013
00:43:20,681 --> 00:43:21,974
{\an8}The soles were too clean?

1014
00:43:22,057 --> 00:43:24,351
{\an8}- They were too clean.
- Did someone change her shoes?

1015
00:43:24,435 --> 00:43:25,686
{\an8}One more thing.

1016
00:43:25,769 --> 00:43:27,521
{\an8}- There's the brick.
- The brick?

1017
00:43:27,605 --> 00:43:30,274
{\an8}I found a brick with blood on it.

1018
00:43:30,357 --> 00:43:32,276
{\an8}A bloody brick?

1019
00:43:32,359 --> 00:43:34,445
{\an8}Yes, I found a brick
stained with a lot of blood.

1020
00:43:34,528 --> 00:43:38,324
{\an8}There's a possibility
she was struck on the head with it.

1021
00:43:38,907 --> 00:43:40,284
{\an8}I don't think so.

1022
00:43:40,367 --> 00:43:42,286
{\an8}That's just what I think.

1023
00:43:42,369 --> 00:43:44,288
{\an8}- Okay.
- Right.

1024
00:43:44,371 --> 00:43:45,289
{\an8}Moving on.

1025
00:43:46,206 --> 00:43:49,043
{\an8}The next person I'm curious about is...

1026
00:43:50,127 --> 00:43:51,795
{\an8}That look on her face
makes me uneasy again.

1027
00:43:52,921 --> 00:43:56,050
{\an8}When else would I get to
go into Gold Spoon's room?

1028
00:43:57,009 --> 00:43:59,136
{\an8}- I'm already uncomfortable.
- She went through everything.

1029
00:43:59,219 --> 00:44:01,555
{\an8}She took advantage of this chance
because she likes him.

1030
00:44:01,639 --> 00:44:05,726
She wouldn't be smiling like that
if she had properly searched his office.

1031
00:44:05,809 --> 00:44:07,728
- I did find something a bit suspicious.
- I see.

1032
00:44:07,811 --> 00:44:09,146
- What is it?
- A bit suspicious?

1033
00:44:09,229 --> 00:44:12,024
{\an8}What was the nature
of your relationship to Gun-mi?

1034
00:44:12,107 --> 00:44:13,025
{\an8}What?

1035
00:44:13,108 --> 00:44:16,737
{\an8}You two were exchanging texts
like you had a thing going on.

1036
00:44:16,820 --> 00:44:21,033
{\an8}Didn't you say
you just met her at the bar?

1037
00:44:21,116 --> 00:44:22,826
{\an8}You guys were texting each other?

1038
00:44:22,910 --> 00:44:26,455
It's not so unusual to exchange numbers
with someone you clicked at a bar, is it?

1039
00:44:26,538 --> 00:44:28,207
You hit her up first.

1040
00:44:28,290 --> 00:44:30,459
I just asked to meet, that's all.

1041
00:44:31,210 --> 00:44:32,294
Explain these, then.

1042
00:44:34,254 --> 00:44:35,506
{\an8}Heart-shaped nostrils!

1043
00:44:36,048 --> 00:44:37,257
{\an8}"Heart-shaped nostrils"?

1044
00:44:37,341 --> 00:44:39,426
Is this why
I don't do it for you, Gold Spoon?

1045
00:44:39,510 --> 00:44:40,636
Jeez, well...

1046
00:44:41,345 --> 00:44:43,305
-"Heart-shaped nostrils"?
- To give you more context,

1047
00:44:43,389 --> 00:44:49,061
he kept photos of heart-shaped nostrils
hidden under his keyboard.

1048
00:44:49,144 --> 00:44:52,981
I mean, everyone has
a favorite body part, no?

1049
00:44:53,065 --> 00:44:54,316
{\an8}- Not really.
- But nostrils?

1050
00:44:54,400 --> 00:44:55,984
{\an8}- Who likes nostrils?
- Come on.

1051
00:44:56,068 --> 00:44:58,153
When you meet someone,
what makes you fall in love with them?

1052
00:44:58,237 --> 00:45:01,448
You can fall for someone's eyes,
personality, or voice.

1053
00:45:01,532 --> 00:45:03,283
Sure, but not nostrils.

1054
00:45:03,367 --> 00:45:06,245
{\an8}- Well, I--
- Anyway, you have a thing for nostrils.

1055
00:45:06,328 --> 00:45:07,746
{\an8}- I just--
- To each their own.

1056
00:45:07,830 --> 00:45:10,332
{\an8}- I wasn't going to say this, but listen.
- Let me see your nostrils.

1057
00:45:10,416 --> 00:45:11,583
{\an8}Have you ever wondered

1058
00:45:11,667 --> 00:45:13,293
{\an8}why some mayo bottles
have a star-shaped tip?

1059
00:45:13,377 --> 00:45:14,378
{\an8}Oh, yeah.

1060
00:45:14,461 --> 00:45:17,464
{\an8}Have you ever imagined air coming out
of your nose in the shape of a heart?

1061
00:45:18,132 --> 00:45:19,883
{\an8}- How beautiful is that?
- I suppose.

1062
00:45:19,967 --> 00:45:21,760
He got all misty-eyed. He's serious.

1063
00:45:22,594 --> 00:45:24,471
- Gold Spoon.
- Do we really need to

1064
00:45:24,555 --> 00:45:26,890
put my personal preference on trial
in front of everyone?

1065
00:45:27,391 --> 00:45:29,268
{\an8}Gun-mi also had heart-shaped nostrils.

1066
00:45:29,351 --> 00:45:31,895
{\an8}- I should check the nostrils later.
- To be honest,

1067
00:45:32,563 --> 00:45:34,314
{\an8}I couldn't sense any love

1068
00:45:34,398 --> 00:45:36,316
{\an8}in the air that came out of your nose.

1069
00:45:37,317 --> 00:45:41,155
{\an8}- What about Lee Gun-mi?
- Breathe out your nose, not your mouth.

1070
00:45:41,238 --> 00:45:42,698
{\an8}Did you feel love from her breath?

1071
00:45:42,781 --> 00:45:43,782
{\an8}It was overwhelming.

1072
00:45:45,909 --> 00:45:48,328
{\an8}Let's move on from that for now.

1073
00:45:48,412 --> 00:45:50,581
{\an8}I noticed you were a regular
at Sensibility Bar.

1074
00:45:50,664 --> 00:45:51,874
{\an8}You even brought back lighters.

1075
00:45:51,957 --> 00:45:54,001
{\an8}- I wasn't a regular.
- There was a business card too.

1076
00:45:54,084 --> 00:45:57,129
{\an8}You clearly go to bars often.

1077
00:45:57,212 --> 00:45:59,465
{\an8}Do you enjoy the nightlife, Gold Spoon?

1078
00:45:59,548 --> 00:46:02,509
{\an8}His family has three generations
of attorneys or something, right?

1079
00:46:02,593 --> 00:46:04,678
{\an8}- That's what he said.
- Won't they lose face if word gets out?

1080
00:46:04,762 --> 00:46:07,973
{\an8}My grandfather
was the Minister of Justice,

1081
00:46:08,056 --> 00:46:09,725
{\an8}and my father was the prosecutor general

1082
00:46:09,808 --> 00:46:11,852
{\an8}of the Seoul Central District
Prosecutors' Office.

1083
00:46:11,935 --> 00:46:15,731
As you know, I care deeply about
the local economy of Gangdam District.

1084
00:46:15,814 --> 00:46:18,192
- That's true. He's a brilliant man.
- I sometimes go to bars

1085
00:46:18,275 --> 00:46:21,153
to get insight into the reality
of self-employed people.

1086
00:46:21,236 --> 00:46:23,071
- Besides, I'm still young.
- What an excuse you've got.

1087
00:46:23,155 --> 00:46:25,365
He goes to the hottest places though,

1088
00:46:25,449 --> 00:46:27,618
{\an8}like the bar run by Kim Piano Key.

1089
00:46:27,701 --> 00:46:28,994
{\an8}Is it still in business?

1090
00:46:29,077 --> 00:46:30,496
{\an8}They say it's a great place for singing.

1091
00:46:31,079 --> 00:46:32,247
{\an8}<i>Kim Piano Key</i>

1092
00:46:32,331 --> 00:46:34,708
{\an8}- I hear he's a pervert.
- Stop bringing up stuff like that.

1093
00:46:34,792 --> 00:46:36,668
{\an8}Anyway, Gold Spoon,

1094
00:46:36,752 --> 00:46:39,296
{\an8}your father must've found out
what you were up to.

1095
00:46:39,379 --> 00:46:40,756
{\an8}Don't screw up.

1096
00:46:40,839 --> 00:46:42,049
{\an8}- Don't screw up!
- Don't screw up.

1097
00:46:42,132 --> 00:46:43,717
{\an8}Gold Spoon, don't screw up!

1098
00:46:43,801 --> 00:46:44,927
{\an8}You get that a lot, don't you?

1099
00:46:45,010 --> 00:46:47,179
You need to get into politics,

1100
00:46:47,262 --> 00:46:48,639
so you were told not to screw up.

1101
00:46:48,722 --> 00:46:51,683
"Don't embarrass your father.
Don't screw up."

1102
00:46:51,767 --> 00:46:54,978
{\an8}What he meant was that
I should take each step with care.

1103
00:46:55,062 --> 00:46:57,356
{\an8}He wasn't trying to set me straight

1104
00:46:57,439 --> 00:46:59,566
{\an8}because I had screwed up in any way.

1105
00:46:59,650 --> 00:47:02,820
{\an8}I also found non-disclosure agreements.

1106
00:47:02,903 --> 00:47:04,404
{\an8}Those who signed them

1107
00:47:04,488 --> 00:47:07,950
{\an8}had to keep conversations
or their relationship with you a secret.

1108
00:47:08,033 --> 00:47:09,326
{\an8}- And who signed them?
- And--

1109
00:47:09,409 --> 00:47:12,663
{\an8}Shin Fox, Bang Libertine,
and Park Hyeon-jun.

1110
00:47:12,746 --> 00:47:15,207
{\an8}- Those were their names.
- So, women?

1111
00:47:15,290 --> 00:47:18,794
{\an8}Most sound like women's names,
but Park Hyeon-jun sounds like a man's.

1112
00:47:18,877 --> 00:47:21,296
{\an8}Wait, so were you
in a romantic relationship with them?

1113
00:47:21,380 --> 00:47:23,674
{\an8}Well, I'm not married.

1114
00:47:24,341 --> 00:47:27,177
{\an8}I have both time and money,
so I can date whoever I want.

1115
00:47:27,261 --> 00:47:28,428
{\an8}Do you swing both ways?

1116
00:47:29,471 --> 00:47:30,722
{\an8}Gender isn't important.

1117
00:47:30,806 --> 00:47:33,725
{\an8}- I date whoever I'm attracted to.
- Oh, so it doesn't matter to you.

1118
00:47:33,809 --> 00:47:35,561
{\an8}- Gender doesn't matter?
- Does it not matter to you too?

1119
00:47:35,644 --> 00:47:36,645
{\an8}I guess that's possible for some.

1120
00:47:36,728 --> 00:47:39,731
{\an8}I can be drawn to anyone,
and that can sometimes leave me

1121
00:47:39,815 --> 00:47:42,526
{\an8}vulnerable to people
who want to exploit my wealth.

1122
00:47:42,609 --> 00:47:43,986
{\an8}- So that's why.
- He's an attorney for sure.

1123
00:47:44,069 --> 00:47:46,947
{\an8}It's to keep them quiet
if they change their minds later.

1124
00:47:47,030 --> 00:47:48,949
{\an8}- It's like an insurance, huh?
- You could say that.

1125
00:47:49,032 --> 00:47:51,660
{\an8}- Detective, here's what I want to know.
- Go on.

1126
00:47:51,743 --> 00:47:53,745
{\an8}Was he dating Lee Gun-mi

1127
00:47:53,829 --> 00:47:55,205
{\an8}or Lee Gun-jun?

1128
00:47:55,289 --> 00:47:56,623
{\an8}That's a good question.

1129
00:47:56,707 --> 00:47:58,667
{\an8}I'm curious about that too.

1130
00:47:58,750 --> 00:48:00,627
{\an8}In your messages with Park Hyeon-jun,

1131
00:48:01,420 --> 00:48:06,091
{\an8}there was a photo of you two in bed,
topless and under the covers.

1132
00:48:06,174 --> 00:48:07,551
{\an8}It must've been chilly.

1133
00:48:08,385 --> 00:48:11,597
{\an8}Tell us about the unforgettable
memories of that day, Gold Spoon.

1134
00:48:11,680 --> 00:48:14,099
{\an8}You sure looked like you had a good time.

1135
00:48:15,100 --> 00:48:16,268
I'll cover my ears.

1136
00:48:16,351 --> 00:48:19,062
- Did you sleep with him?
- He's looking away, avoiding eye contact.

1137
00:48:19,146 --> 00:48:20,939
- I'll tell you.
- Did you sleep with him?

1138
00:48:22,816 --> 00:48:24,192
{\an8}I had a drink

1139
00:48:24,276 --> 00:48:25,819
{\an8}- and passed out.
- As if.

1140
00:48:27,487 --> 00:48:30,115
{\an8}Didn't I explain
the non-disclosure agreements?

1141
00:48:30,198 --> 00:48:31,742
{\an8}That man in the photo

1142
00:48:32,367 --> 00:48:34,995
{\an8}stole the document from me.

1143
00:48:35,078 --> 00:48:36,413
{\an8}While you were out cold, huh?

1144
00:48:36,496 --> 00:48:37,623
{\an8}Gold Spoon.

1145
00:48:37,706 --> 00:48:38,582
{\an8}Go on.

1146
00:48:38,665 --> 00:48:40,417
{\an8}I have shocking evidence.

1147
00:48:40,500 --> 00:48:41,335
{\an8}- What is it?
- Is that so?

1148
00:48:41,919 --> 00:48:45,756
{\an8}The man in the photo
looks very much like Gun-mi.

1149
00:48:45,839 --> 00:48:47,132
{\an8}Is this Gun-mi?

1150
00:48:47,716 --> 00:48:48,675
{\an8}Who is he?

1151
00:48:48,759 --> 00:48:51,428
{\an8}Let me back you up with what I found.

1152
00:48:51,511 --> 00:48:53,805
{\an8}- Sure.
- This was on Gun-mi's phone.

1153
00:48:53,889 --> 00:48:55,098
{\an8}Didn't you catch this?

1154
00:48:55,182 --> 00:48:56,266
{\an8}I didn't.

1155
00:48:56,350 --> 00:48:57,935
{\an8}I found text messages on the phone

1156
00:48:58,018 --> 00:48:59,895
{\an8}- I read those texts too.
- that was on her body.

1157
00:48:59,978 --> 00:49:04,316
{\an8}In Mr. Gold Spoon's phone,
the name was saved as Park Hyeon-jun.

1158
00:49:04,399 --> 00:49:05,692
{\an8}However,

1159
00:49:05,776 --> 00:49:09,363
{\an8}an identical string of texts
was found on Gun-mi's phone.

1160
00:49:09,446 --> 00:49:10,864
{\an8}Do you see where I'm going with this?

1161
00:49:10,948 --> 00:49:14,159
{\an8}I'm afraid you're going to have to
give us an explanation.

1162
00:49:14,993 --> 00:49:17,079
{\an8}Gun-mi and I had agreed to meet.

1163
00:49:17,162 --> 00:49:20,749
{\an8}But after a while,
she began playing hard to get.

1164
00:49:20,832 --> 00:49:21,667
{\an8}Right.

1165
00:49:21,750 --> 00:49:24,920
{\an8}She kept turning down plans to meet,
so I got upset.

1166
00:49:25,003 --> 00:49:27,881
{\an8}By now, you all know
why I was drawn to her.

1167
00:49:27,965 --> 00:49:29,424
{\an8}Yeah, the nostrils.

1168
00:49:29,508 --> 00:49:33,595
{\an8}That's when I met Park Hyeon-jun,
who looked a lot like Gun-mi

1169
00:49:33,679 --> 00:49:36,014
{\an8}and had the feature I loved.

1170
00:49:36,098 --> 00:49:37,224
{\an8}RAN INTO PARK HYEON-JUN
AT THE CONVENIENCE STORE

1171
00:49:37,307 --> 00:49:39,059
{\an8}I felt drawn to him too.

1172
00:49:39,142 --> 00:49:41,311
{\an8}So I asked for his number,

1173
00:49:41,395 --> 00:49:42,688
{\an8}and we began exchanging texts.

1174
00:49:43,772 --> 00:49:46,525
{\an8}But then you realized
they were the same person.

1175
00:49:46,608 --> 00:49:47,609
{\an8}I had no idea.

1176
00:49:48,777 --> 00:49:51,029
{\an8}- He was Gun-jun.
- You were duped twice.

1177
00:49:51,113 --> 00:49:52,864
{\an8}It was Gun-jun all along.

1178
00:49:52,948 --> 00:49:55,701
Heart-shaped nostrils
can't be that common.

1179
00:49:55,784 --> 00:49:57,661
And of course, he'd use a fake name.

1180
00:49:57,744 --> 00:49:59,538
You and Gun-jun got into bed, and--

1181
00:49:59,621 --> 00:50:02,582
{\an8}Before today, I had never even
heard of the name Lee Gun-jun.

1182
00:50:02,666 --> 00:50:04,543
{\an8}- Hyeon-jun was Gun-jun.
- Park Hyeon-jun, then.

1183
00:50:04,626 --> 00:50:06,920
{\an8}- Here's one last thing, Gold Spoon.
- Sure.

1184
00:50:07,004 --> 00:50:08,839
{\an8}Did you ever throw away...

1185
00:50:09,840 --> 00:50:11,049
{\an8}something important?

1186
00:50:11,133 --> 00:50:13,885
{\an8}He likely threw it away
because it was trash. Come on, Dancer.

1187
00:50:13,969 --> 00:50:15,804
{\an8}My love for you hasn't been crumpled.

1188
00:50:16,471 --> 00:50:17,347
{\an8}What's that?

1189
00:50:17,431 --> 00:50:19,474
{\an8}- The letter I wrote to him.
- You dug that out of the trash?

1190
00:50:19,558 --> 00:50:22,894
{\an8}Did Gold Spoon crumple up
and throw away the love letter

1191
00:50:22,978 --> 00:50:24,563
{\an8}An Dancer wrote him?

1192
00:50:24,646 --> 00:50:26,690
{\an8}An Dancer doesn't have
heart-shaped nostrils,

1193
00:50:26,773 --> 00:50:29,359
{\an8}but she wrote the letter
on a piece of heart-shaped paper.

1194
00:50:29,443 --> 00:50:30,360
{\an8}All right, then.

1195
00:50:30,444 --> 00:50:35,115
{\an8}I think it'd be best
for Gold Spoon to go next.

1196
00:50:35,198 --> 00:50:37,743
{\an8}Before diving into the details,

1197
00:50:38,535 --> 00:50:39,661
{\an8}I'd like to offer

1198
00:50:39,745 --> 00:50:41,455
{\an8}a different interpretation
of the crime scene.

1199
00:50:41,538 --> 00:50:45,000
{\an8}I can't take him seriously anymore
now that I know about his nostril fetish.

1200
00:50:45,709 --> 00:50:48,003
{\an8}Here's the bloodstained brick.

1201
00:50:48,086 --> 00:50:51,339
{\an8}Did anyone happen to see
the fan guard next to it?

1202
00:50:51,423 --> 00:50:52,591
{\an8}- Yes, I saw the fan guard.
- I saw it too.

1203
00:50:52,674 --> 00:50:53,508
{\an8}You saw it, right?

1204
00:50:53,592 --> 00:50:57,345
{\an8}Now, focus on the marks on her back,
which follow an unknown pattern.

1205
00:50:57,429 --> 00:51:01,683
{\an8}When I held the fan guard up to it,
I noticed the patterns matched.

1206
00:51:01,767 --> 00:51:03,435
{\an8}So I made the assumption

1207
00:51:03,518 --> 00:51:07,397
{\an8}that Lee Gun-mi
fell onto the fan guard first,

1208
00:51:07,481 --> 00:51:11,485
{\an8}and someone came up and strangled her,
adding more pressure

1209
00:51:11,568 --> 00:51:14,821
{\an8}and leaving those marks on her back.

1210
00:51:15,405 --> 00:51:17,657
{\an8}And as for the order
in which the murder took place,

1211
00:51:17,741 --> 00:51:20,202
{\an8}I believe she was first attacked
with the brick,

1212
00:51:20,285 --> 00:51:24,414
{\an8}then strangled,
which left those marks on her back.

1213
00:51:24,498 --> 00:51:26,333
{\an8}That's my theory about the crime.

1214
00:51:26,416 --> 00:51:29,127
{\an8}Mr. Attorney sure is smart, isn't he?

1215
00:51:29,211 --> 00:51:31,088
{\an8}Nobody else thought of that.

1216
00:51:31,171 --> 00:51:34,424
{\an8}Mr. Club, I'm sure you're aware

1217
00:51:34,508 --> 00:51:36,009
{\an8}that I'm not that fond of you.

1218
00:51:36,718 --> 00:51:38,470
{\an8}- Excuse me?
- There's something sketchy about you.

1219
00:51:38,553 --> 00:51:41,556
{\an8}- You're harsh with your employees.
- That came out of nowhere.

1220
00:51:41,640 --> 00:51:45,227
{\an8}So I went to his office at the club
to dig up dirt on him.

1221
00:51:45,310 --> 00:51:47,646
{\an8}But more so than Mr. Club,

1222
00:51:47,729 --> 00:51:52,442
{\an8}Ms. An Dancer surprised me
with an unexpectedly dark past.

1223
00:51:52,526 --> 00:51:53,860
{\an8}- Is that so?
- Yes.

1224
00:51:53,944 --> 00:51:56,321
{\an8}I'm getting a headache. She's too much.

1225
00:51:56,404 --> 00:51:58,824
{\an8}No, Dancer.
I told you to hold the heart up here.

1226
00:52:02,744 --> 00:52:05,664
{\an8}- I think he kind of liked that.
- And breathe out of your nose.

1227
00:52:05,747 --> 00:52:06,748
{\an8}Ms. An Dancer,

1228
00:52:06,832 --> 00:52:10,085
{\an8}aren't you stuck in
an exploitative contract with Club Slam?

1229
00:52:10,168 --> 00:52:12,295
{\an8}Come on, it's hardly exploitative.

1230
00:52:12,379 --> 00:52:15,215
{\an8}Like I'd sign one with a mere dancer.
She's not even a K-pop idol.

1231
00:52:15,298 --> 00:52:19,344
{\an8}Ms. Dancer, you were a trainee
at Star-Star Entertainment, right?

1232
00:52:19,427 --> 00:52:20,303
{\an8}That's right.

1233
00:52:20,387 --> 00:52:22,264
{\an8}When she signed with Club Slam,

1234
00:52:22,347 --> 00:52:25,600
{\an8}her debt was carried over and increased
to a total of 550 million won.

1235
00:52:25,684 --> 00:52:27,769
{\an8}That alone makes it
an exploitative contract.

1236
00:52:27,853 --> 00:52:28,895
{\an8}On top of that,

1237
00:52:28,979 --> 00:52:30,522
{\an8}one of the clauses

1238
00:52:30,605 --> 00:52:33,066
{\an8}states that they could take measures
to make her look like her old self.

1239
00:52:33,150 --> 00:52:34,609
{\an8}- Have you seen her old photos?
- Oh dear.

1240
00:52:34,693 --> 00:52:36,570
{\an8}No, don't even bring them out.

1241
00:52:36,653 --> 00:52:38,321
{\an8}Don't bring them out.
It's best not to see them.

1242
00:52:38,405 --> 00:52:39,239
{\an8}Gold Spoon!

1243
00:52:39,322 --> 00:52:41,700
{\an8}- Cover your eyes.
- Don't touch anything!

1244
00:52:41,783 --> 00:52:46,329
{\an8}It's such a shame
that they butchered her pretty face.

1245
00:52:47,497 --> 00:52:48,331
{\an8}An Dancer...

1246
00:52:48,832 --> 00:52:49,791
{\an8}GASPING

1247
00:52:50,500 --> 00:52:51,626
{\an8}What's this?

1248
00:52:54,171 --> 00:52:55,964
{\an8}Is this what An Dancer looked like before?

1249
00:52:58,967 --> 00:53:02,512
{\an8}- I thought it was you, Detective.
- Look, Detective.

1250
00:53:02,596 --> 00:53:04,556
{\an8}- Your eyes are no good.
- I thought she looked amazing.

1251
00:53:05,432 --> 00:53:08,268
{\an8}- I have a little question, Mr. Attorney.
- What is it?

1252
00:53:08,351 --> 00:53:10,687
{\an8}What if she had that face

1253
00:53:10,770 --> 00:53:13,106
{\an8}- but with heart-shaped nostrils?
- I've been wondering that too.

1254
00:53:13,190 --> 00:53:15,275
{\an8}- Gold Spoon, is that all it takes?
- Would you date her or not?

1255
00:53:15,358 --> 00:53:16,484
{\an8}Then I'd date her.

1256
00:53:16,568 --> 00:53:18,737
{\an8}Really? Is the fetish really that intense?

1257
00:53:18,820 --> 00:53:20,447
{\an8}- You should go back to your old self.
- It's my preference.

1258
00:53:20,530 --> 00:53:22,199
{\an8}And while you're at it,
get your nostrils done too.

1259
00:53:22,282 --> 00:53:24,117
{\an8}- Gold Spoon.
- All right.

1260
00:53:24,201 --> 00:53:27,412
{\an8}I think it was the vanity.
That's where I found her phone.

1261
00:53:27,495 --> 00:53:29,122
{\an8}These are some of her texts.

1262
00:53:29,206 --> 00:53:30,916
{\an8}She and Mr. Club texted.

1263
00:53:30,999 --> 00:53:33,251
"Mr. Kim, I have something important
to tell you after work."

1264
00:53:33,335 --> 00:53:34,377
She sent that on August 12th.

1265
00:53:35,003 --> 00:53:37,422
I wondered what she wanted to
talk to him about.

1266
00:53:37,505 --> 00:53:40,717
{\an8}You were halfway through completing
a contract termination.

1267
00:53:40,800 --> 00:53:42,510
{\an8}Is that so?

1268
00:53:42,594 --> 00:53:45,555
{\an8}- Dancer, is this true?
- I found the contents surprising.

1269
00:53:45,639 --> 00:53:46,640
It said that you repaid

1270
00:53:46,723 --> 00:53:48,642
the 500-million-won debt
in a single payment

1271
00:53:48,725 --> 00:53:50,185
- and demanded termination.
- That's right.

1272
00:53:50,268 --> 00:53:52,020
- She paid off her debt already?
- She didn't.

1273
00:53:52,103 --> 00:53:55,440
The only signature on it was hers.

1274
00:53:55,523 --> 00:53:58,735
- She was likely expecting a large payment.
- You think so?

1275
00:53:58,818 --> 00:53:59,819
- An Dancer.
- Yes?

1276
00:53:59,903 --> 00:54:01,988
{\an8}Where were you going to
get the money from?

1277
00:54:02,072 --> 00:54:04,658
{\an8}- I've got my own plans, Mr. Kim.
- Of course.

1278
00:54:04,741 --> 00:54:06,743
What I really want to know
is where she got the money

1279
00:54:06,826 --> 00:54:09,579
to settle her debt and request
to terminate her contract.

1280
00:54:10,163 --> 00:54:11,790
Then, I found this in her desk drawer.

1281
00:54:11,873 --> 00:54:13,959
You didn't have to be so thorough,
Gold Spoon.

1282
00:54:14,542 --> 00:54:17,128
{\an8}She had flyers
and a business card from China.

1283
00:54:17,212 --> 00:54:19,089
{\an8}- China?
- The name on it was Lee Eighty.

1284
00:54:19,172 --> 00:54:20,048
{\an8}The note in the back read,

1285
00:54:20,131 --> 00:54:22,550
"1 billion, 500 million"
and something about debuting in China.

1286
00:54:22,634 --> 00:54:24,970
The tone was rather hopeful.

1287
00:54:25,053 --> 00:54:28,598
I couldn't tell what was on the flyers
because they were all in Chinese,

1288
00:54:28,682 --> 00:54:30,392
{\an8}so I ran them through a translation app,

1289
00:54:30,976 --> 00:54:34,104
{\an8}and I realized
they were VIP party exposés.

1290
00:54:34,938 --> 00:54:36,356
"The fall of the aristocracy."

1291
00:54:36,439 --> 00:54:39,150
"The disgraceful truth
behind elite political families."

1292
00:54:39,234 --> 00:54:42,112
The other one exposed

1293
00:54:42,195 --> 00:54:46,241
the top celebrity, Kang Pung-pung,
as a drug addict.

1294
00:54:46,324 --> 00:54:48,118
- And this.
- Yes?

1295
00:54:48,201 --> 00:54:49,661
You had a tiny camera.

1296
00:54:49,744 --> 00:54:52,038
{\an8}- A camera?
- There was a camera

1297
00:54:52,122 --> 00:54:53,707
{\an8}smaller than a table tennis ball.

1298
00:54:53,790 --> 00:54:54,874
{\an8}By any chance,

1299
00:54:54,958 --> 00:54:58,336
{\an8}were you planning to film me secretly
and sell the video to a Chinese broker?

1300
00:54:58,420 --> 00:55:00,463
{\an8}Is that how you were going to
make the money?

1301
00:55:00,547 --> 00:55:03,675
{\an8}- Well, I mean...
- Wait, you don't know?

1302
00:55:03,758 --> 00:55:05,719
{\an8}The grown-ups are talking, all right?

1303
00:55:05,802 --> 00:55:06,803
{\an8}I'm just...

1304
00:55:07,345 --> 00:55:08,680
{\an8}Do you really not know?

1305
00:55:08,763 --> 00:55:12,183
{\an8}- What is it? Tell us.
- The grown-ups are talking.

1306
00:55:12,267 --> 00:55:14,102
{\an8}I'm just curious, that's all.

1307
00:55:14,185 --> 00:55:16,271
{\an8}- Are you serious right now?
- Seriously?

1308
00:55:16,354 --> 00:55:19,441
{\an8}Anyway, that's what I found out
about An Dancer.

1309
00:55:19,524 --> 00:55:21,151
{\an8}- That'll be all.
- All right.

1310
00:55:21,735 --> 00:55:22,652
{\an8}Should I go next?

1311
00:55:22,736 --> 00:55:23,611
{\an8}- Sure.
- Go ahead.

1312
00:55:23,695 --> 00:55:25,071
{\an8}NEXT BRIEFING: KIM CLUB

1313
00:55:25,155 --> 00:55:26,698
{\an8}Mr. Attorney.

1314
00:55:26,781 --> 00:55:27,699
{\an8}Me, again?

1315
00:55:27,782 --> 00:55:30,368
{\an8}Did you check the wallet
on the victim's body?

1316
00:55:30,952 --> 00:55:31,953
{\an8}- The wallet.
- I did.

1317
00:55:32,037 --> 00:55:35,081
{\an8}Inside the wallet
was a check for 50 million won.

1318
00:55:35,165 --> 00:55:37,042
{\an8}Wasn't that issued by Mr. Gold Spoon?

1319
00:55:37,125 --> 00:55:38,335
{\an8}- It was.
- That's right.

1320
00:55:38,418 --> 00:55:40,295
{\an8}It's likely that Lee Gun-mi

1321
00:55:40,378 --> 00:55:43,006
{\an8}blackmailed him using
a photo of them together in bed.

1322
00:55:43,089 --> 00:55:46,509
{\an8}That seems to be how she got the money.

1323
00:55:47,177 --> 00:55:48,720
{\an8}I'm a victim too, guys.

1324
00:55:48,803 --> 00:55:50,889
{\an8}- That came out of nowhere.
- That was unexpected.

1325
00:55:50,972 --> 00:55:53,767
- What's with the sudden 180?
- That 50 million won?

1326
00:55:53,850 --> 00:55:56,269
I should've paid five million won
and earned her silence,

1327
00:55:56,853 --> 00:55:58,396
{\an8}but I failed to do that.

1328
00:55:58,480 --> 00:56:00,273
{\an8}So I had to pay an additional 50 million.

1329
00:56:00,357 --> 00:56:02,233
{\an8}PARK HYEON-JUN/CONVENIENCE STORE
I TOOK IT THAT DAY

1330
00:56:02,317 --> 00:56:05,070
If only it ended there, right?
But I'm sure it didn't.

1331
00:56:05,153 --> 00:56:06,696
And that usually leads to murder.

1332
00:56:06,780 --> 00:56:08,948
{\an8}Fifty million won is just the beginning.

1333
00:56:09,032 --> 00:56:10,909
{\an8}The grown-ups are talking!

1334
00:56:12,911 --> 00:56:13,787
{\an8}HURT

1335
00:56:13,870 --> 00:56:15,121
{\an8}Come on, C-store.

1336
00:56:15,205 --> 00:56:18,666
{\an8}Look at C-store,
acting all innocent and naive.

1337
00:56:18,750 --> 00:56:20,752
{\an8}- Why? I thought he was hardworking.
- I am.

1338
00:56:20,835 --> 00:56:24,297
{\an8}Your past is quite different
from your present, C-store.

1339
00:56:24,381 --> 00:56:26,216
{\an8}- Is that so?
- What? What do you mean?

1340
00:56:26,299 --> 00:56:27,592
{\an8}- Turns out,
- Yes?

1341
00:56:27,675 --> 00:56:29,511
{\an8}C-store spent a year in juvie.

1342
00:56:29,594 --> 00:56:31,388
{\an8}- In juvenile detention?
- I knew it.

1343
00:56:31,471 --> 00:56:33,598
{\an8}It's rare to be sent to juvie for a year.

1344
00:56:33,681 --> 00:56:38,186
{\an8}He made Lee Gun-jun dress up as a girl
and bring him money.

1345
00:56:38,269 --> 00:56:39,437
{\an8}He was the perpetrator.

1346
00:56:39,521 --> 00:56:42,190
{\an8}- Man, what a piece of trash.
- So, it's like this.

1347
00:56:42,273 --> 00:56:44,901
{\an8}So he knew about the victim's proclivity
and took advantage of it.

1348
00:56:44,984 --> 00:56:47,987
{\an8}- That might not have even been the case.
- He's the worst, Detective.

1349
00:56:48,071 --> 00:56:49,739
{\an8}He might not even have had
such a proclivity,

1350
00:56:49,823 --> 00:56:51,616
{\an8}but the experience of making money
under such a disguise

1351
00:56:51,699 --> 00:56:54,327
{\an8}could've led him to further pursue it.

1352
00:56:54,411 --> 00:56:55,286
{\an8}You tell us.

1353
00:56:55,870 --> 00:56:57,122
{\an8}Talk, you piece of trash!

1354
00:56:57,205 --> 00:57:00,166
{\an8}How dare you move to our neighborhood,
acting like some innocent kid?

1355
00:57:00,250 --> 00:57:01,751
{\an8}You can STFU.

1356
00:57:02,377 --> 00:57:06,256
{\an8}- I didn't know you knew such a term.
- Is that the best abbreviation you know?

1357
00:57:06,881 --> 00:57:08,425
{\an8}- C-store.
- I know a few.

1358
00:57:08,508 --> 00:57:10,593
{\an8}What did I tell you
about how you talk to your elders?

1359
00:57:11,886 --> 00:57:13,304
{\an8}- You brat.
- Let me explain.

1360
00:57:13,388 --> 00:57:14,806
{\an8}You see,

1361
00:57:14,889 --> 00:57:15,932
{\an8}back in the day,

1362
00:57:16,850 --> 00:57:18,184
{\an8}I used to have some fun.

1363
00:57:18,268 --> 00:57:20,895
{\an8}- Were you a bully?
- He was a short and skinny kid,

1364
00:57:20,979 --> 00:57:22,814
{\an8}so we made him do things for us.

1365
00:57:22,897 --> 00:57:25,775
{\an8}But I didn't know about this side of him.

1366
00:57:25,859 --> 00:57:27,277
{\an8}I only found out today.

1367
00:57:27,360 --> 00:57:31,281
{\an8}I had no idea that Gun-jun
spent part of his life as Lee Gun-mi.

1368
00:57:31,364 --> 00:57:32,574
{\an8}- I swear.
- Is that so?

1369
00:57:32,657 --> 00:57:35,076
{\an8}Detective, can I step in
and share something?

1370
00:57:35,160 --> 00:57:36,828
{\an8}- I'm probably the only one who knows it.
- Sure.

1371
00:57:36,911 --> 00:57:41,040
{\an8}As I said, Gun-mi and I
have been friends for a few years.

1372
00:57:41,124 --> 00:57:44,502
{\an8}She appeared in Gangdam out of nowhere
after being gone for several years,

1373
00:57:44,586 --> 00:57:46,713
{\an8}so I wondered why.

1374
00:57:46,796 --> 00:57:49,757
{\an8}Then one day, she told me

1375
00:57:49,841 --> 00:57:53,303
{\an8}- that she had to get revenge on someone.
- Revenge?

1376
00:57:53,386 --> 00:57:54,471
{\an8}- Was this recent?
- Yes.

1377
00:57:55,054 --> 00:57:57,599
{\an8}And she said she was going to
leave this place once she was done.

1378
00:57:57,682 --> 00:57:58,892
{\an8}Is that so?

1379
00:57:58,975 --> 00:58:00,518
{\an8}- Lee Gun-mi said that?
- Maybe it was this bastard.

1380
00:58:01,352 --> 00:58:04,522
{\an8}- There's something off about him.
- I'll keep that in mind.

1381
00:58:04,606 --> 00:58:07,025
{\an8}Anyway, I'm sure you all saw
the hole in the skull.

1382
00:58:07,108 --> 00:58:08,109
{\an8}- Yes.
- Yes.

1383
00:58:08,193 --> 00:58:13,323
{\an8}I found something that appears
to match the size and depth of the wound.

1384
00:58:13,406 --> 00:58:15,700
{\an8}A claw hammer
was in the convenience store.

1385
00:58:16,618 --> 00:58:17,577
{\an8}In the convenience store?

1386
00:58:18,161 --> 00:58:19,162
{\an8}So the weapon wasn't the brick?

1387
00:58:19,245 --> 00:58:22,415
{\an8}- That seems like the same size.
- It's the perfect size.

1388
00:58:22,499 --> 00:58:23,750
{\an8}And here's more about his past.

1389
00:58:23,833 --> 00:58:24,876
{\an8}Do you...

1390
00:58:25,543 --> 00:58:27,045
{\an8}have a kid?

1391
00:58:28,838 --> 00:58:29,964
Look here.

1392
00:58:30,048 --> 00:58:32,300
Here's C-store in his school uniform.

1393
00:58:32,383 --> 00:58:34,511
Next to him is his girlfriend
who's also in a school uniform.

1394
00:58:34,594 --> 00:58:37,013
But that girlfriend is holding a baby.

1395
00:58:37,096 --> 00:58:41,518
Next to them in the photo
is an older man who looks very angry.

1396
00:58:41,601 --> 00:58:43,686
- They were teen parents.
- Teen parents.

1397
00:58:43,770 --> 00:58:47,232
I was clueless and didn't know better,

1398
00:58:47,315 --> 00:58:50,109
{\an8}so I ended up becoming a dad
in high school.

1399
00:58:50,193 --> 00:58:52,195
{\an8}But I took responsibility.

1400
00:58:52,278 --> 00:58:55,156
{\an8}I've accepted responsibility
and stayed committed.

1401
00:58:55,240 --> 00:58:57,325
{\an8}The older man in the photo
is my father-in-law.

1402
00:58:57,408 --> 00:58:58,910
{\an8}He owns the convenience store.

1403
00:58:58,993 --> 00:59:00,745
{\an8}Oh, it's your father-in-law's
convenience store.

1404
00:59:00,828 --> 00:59:02,413
{\an8}- That's right.
- Really?

1405
00:59:02,497 --> 00:59:04,374
{\an8}- No wonder.
- Oh dear.

1406
00:59:04,457 --> 00:59:05,583
{\an8}And so--

1407
00:59:05,667 --> 00:59:07,961
{\an8}No wonder he never took any days off.

1408
00:59:08,044 --> 00:59:09,963
{\an8}- Do you still get paid?
- Of course!

1409
00:59:10,672 --> 00:59:14,634
I didn't understand what this was
because I didn't know who owned the store.

1410
00:59:14,717 --> 00:59:16,511
But his father-in-law

1411
00:59:17,178 --> 00:59:20,932
must've installed a security camera
to monitor him.

1412
00:59:21,015 --> 00:59:23,851
{\an8}I found one in the trash
that had been smashed.

1413
00:59:23,935 --> 00:59:27,105
{\an8}- Yes, I smashed the heck out of it
- You're a piece of work.

1414
00:59:27,188 --> 00:59:29,065
{\an8}after finding it.

1415
00:59:29,148 --> 00:59:31,234
{\an8}You're still just as hot-tempered.

1416
00:59:31,317 --> 00:59:33,194
{\an8}He seems to have anger management issues.

1417
00:59:33,278 --> 00:59:35,113
{\an8}Here's what I think.

1418
00:59:35,780 --> 00:59:39,909
He's been building up anger inside him
like a burning fire.

1419
00:59:39,993 --> 00:59:41,536
When I went through his desk,

1420
00:59:41,619 --> 00:59:44,205
I found news articles
on so-called random acts of violence,

1421
00:59:44,789 --> 00:59:46,624
including an incident
where boiling oil was poured on someone

1422
00:59:46,708 --> 00:59:48,668
and a random stabbing spree.

1423
00:59:48,751 --> 00:59:51,588
He had the news clippings
in his little collection.

1424
00:59:51,671 --> 00:59:52,505
Really?

1425
00:59:52,589 --> 00:59:54,716
{\an8}Did you randomly attack
and kill Lee Gun-mi?

1426
00:59:54,799 --> 00:59:58,011
{\an8}How can it be random
when we knew each other?

1427
00:59:58,094 --> 01:00:00,430
{\an8}You didn't know Lee Gun-mi though.
You knew Lee Gun-jun.

1428
01:00:01,222 --> 01:00:03,641
{\an8}- She's right.
- See? You just got caught in a lie, huh?

1429
01:00:03,725 --> 01:00:06,019
{\an8}It could count as random
because you didn't know Lee Gun-mi.

1430
01:00:06,102 --> 01:00:06,978
{\an8}That's not true.

1431
01:00:07,937 --> 01:00:08,896
- One more thing.
- Unbelievable.

1432
01:00:08,980 --> 01:00:10,690
I haven't quite figured this out yet,

1433
01:00:10,773 --> 01:00:12,108
but on the convenience store counter,

1434
01:00:13,109 --> 01:00:15,612
{\an8}I found five dolls displayed in a row.

1435
01:00:15,695 --> 01:00:18,948
{\an8}- Give me a break.
- They all had long, straight hair

1436
01:00:19,032 --> 01:00:21,618
{\an8}and were holding umbrellas.

1437
01:00:21,701 --> 01:00:23,494
{\an8}- A rainy day.
- Perhaps on a rainy day like today,

1438
01:00:23,578 --> 01:00:25,913
{\an8}he kills someone and gets rid of a doll.

1439
01:00:26,873 --> 01:00:28,791
{\an8}Or maybe he's adding one

1440
01:00:28,875 --> 01:00:30,335
{\an8}- for every crime he commits.
- That may be the case.

1441
01:00:30,418 --> 01:00:32,420
{\an8}You bastard. I've had it with you.

1442
01:00:33,004 --> 01:00:35,757
{\an8}- Assume push-up position.
- My joints on my right side are weak.

1443
01:00:36,424 --> 01:00:37,342
{\an8}Why do you have those dolls?

1444
01:00:37,425 --> 01:00:39,594
{\an8}I just like them, that's all.

1445
01:00:39,677 --> 01:00:41,471
{\an8}Why would you though? You're a guy.

1446
01:00:41,554 --> 01:00:43,431
Please be respectful
of people's preferences.

1447
01:00:43,514 --> 01:00:45,016
- Right.
- Sir, come on.

1448
01:00:45,099 --> 01:00:47,018
I guess it's better than a nostril fetish.

1449
01:00:47,101 --> 01:00:48,519
Of course. His fetish was shocking.

1450
01:00:48,603 --> 01:00:49,979
{\an8}There are no lesser preferences.

1451
01:00:50,063 --> 01:00:52,857
{\an8}- The neighborhood has such an awful vibe.
- All right, Detective.

1452
01:00:52,940 --> 01:00:53,983
{\an8}That's all I have for now.

1453
01:00:54,067 --> 01:00:55,693
{\an8}I'm disappointed in you, Mr. Club.

1454
01:00:55,777 --> 01:00:58,696
{\an8}All right, then.
C-store, why don't you go next?

1455
01:00:58,780 --> 01:01:00,073
{\an8}Sure.

1456
01:01:00,156 --> 01:01:01,032
{\an8}Okay.

1457
01:01:01,115 --> 01:01:02,700
{\an8}First of all,

1458
01:01:02,784 --> 01:01:05,536
{\an8}I went to Mr. Club's club.

1459
01:01:05,620 --> 01:01:07,038
{\an8}It was for one reason only.

1460
01:01:07,121 --> 01:01:08,206
{\an8}You wanted to go inside, huh?

1461
01:01:08,289 --> 01:01:11,417
{\an8}It was amazing. My only regret
is that I couldn't play some music.

1462
01:01:11,501 --> 01:01:14,671
{\an8}Come straight to me next time,
and I'll get you past the bouncer.

1463
01:01:14,754 --> 01:01:16,130
{\an8}Thanks, sir. Okay.

1464
01:01:16,214 --> 01:01:19,967
{\an8}My next stop was Ms. Chicken's restaurant.

1465
01:01:20,051 --> 01:01:22,303
{\an8}All you did at my club
was take a tour of the place?

1466
01:01:22,387 --> 01:01:23,304
{\an8}Yes.

1467
01:01:24,222 --> 01:01:25,431
{\an8}C-store,

1468
01:01:25,515 --> 01:01:26,891
{\an8}assume push-up position.

1469
01:01:29,060 --> 01:01:30,645
{\an8}This is a tough character to play.

1470
01:01:30,728 --> 01:01:33,606
{\an8}I went to Ms. Chicken's restaurant,

1471
01:01:33,690 --> 01:01:35,525
{\an8}and I was so shocked.

1472
01:01:35,608 --> 01:01:37,610
{\an8}Ms. Chicken

1473
01:01:38,361 --> 01:01:39,362
{\an8}used to be

1474
01:01:40,196 --> 01:01:42,198
{\an8}a boxer.

1475
01:01:42,907 --> 01:01:44,075
{\an8}I saw that too.

1476
01:01:45,076 --> 01:01:46,577
{\an8}A former boxer?

1477
01:01:46,661 --> 01:01:48,162
{\an8}- So she was a boxer?
- She was a boxer,

1478
01:01:48,246 --> 01:01:50,081
{\an8}- but not just any boxer.
- Look at her posture.

1479
01:01:50,164 --> 01:01:51,541
{\an8}In 2015,

1480
01:01:51,624 --> 01:01:54,544
{\an8}she even won
the Strong Punch, Fire Fist Award.

1481
01:01:54,627 --> 01:01:57,380
{\an8}So, why didn't her boxing career take off?

1482
01:01:57,463 --> 01:01:58,506
{\an8}I wonder why.

1483
01:01:58,589 --> 01:02:01,884
{\an8}- She had an unfortunate accident.
- Oh dear.

1484
01:02:01,968 --> 01:02:03,845
{\an8}She tore her rotator cuff.

1485
01:02:04,804 --> 01:02:06,723
In her case, it was serious.

1486
01:02:06,806 --> 01:02:08,474
However,

1487
01:02:08,558 --> 01:02:12,103
she didn't consider this a mere accident.

1488
01:02:12,186 --> 01:02:15,481
She claimed that someone had drugged her
before the match.

1489
01:02:16,023 --> 01:02:19,110
Why? Because someone needed her to lose.

1490
01:02:19,193 --> 01:02:22,238
She accused someone of fixing the match

1491
01:02:22,321 --> 01:02:24,949
{\an8}and asked the police
to launch a formal investigation.

1492
01:02:25,032 --> 01:02:28,035
{\an8}However, the police claimed
that there was no evidence of her claims

1493
01:02:28,119 --> 01:02:29,662
{\an8}and closed the case.

1494
01:02:29,746 --> 01:02:31,748
{\an8}Then, she received the paperwork
stating as such.

1495
01:02:31,831 --> 01:02:33,082
{\an8}RE: INVESTIGATION RESULTS NOTICE

1496
01:02:33,166 --> 01:02:35,752
{\an8}- On it, she left a memo.
- What did it say?

1497
01:02:36,627 --> 01:02:39,130
-"I'll get you myself."
- I see.

1498
01:02:39,213 --> 01:02:41,007
- Unbelievable.
- What's wrong with that?

1499
01:02:41,090 --> 01:02:45,553
There are texts she exchanged with Gun-mi
after the case was closed.

1500
01:02:45,636 --> 01:02:46,721
What did they talk about?

1501
01:02:46,804 --> 01:02:49,056
{\an8}-"So, what about your boss?"
- Okay.

1502
01:02:49,140 --> 01:02:52,643
{\an8}There's a big time jump
after the first text.

1503
01:02:52,727 --> 01:02:54,729
{\an8}She asked Gun-mi if she had looked into it

1504
01:02:54,812 --> 01:02:57,023
{\an8}and confronted her for not reporting back.

1505
01:02:57,106 --> 01:03:00,276
{\an8}Gun-mi appeared to be very stressed by it.

1506
01:03:00,359 --> 01:03:02,069
{\an8}- Now, let's talk about this.
- Detective, hold on.

1507
01:03:02,153 --> 01:03:05,531
{\an8}What was Gun-mi doing in 2015

1508
01:03:05,615 --> 01:03:07,408
{\an8}for you to be interested in her boss?

1509
01:03:08,659 --> 01:03:11,078
{\an8}She was the one
who injected the drug into my shoulder.

1510
01:03:11,162 --> 01:03:12,580
{\an8}- Really?
- What?

1511
01:03:12,663 --> 01:03:16,209
Before the match, a girl bumped into me
and injected something into my shoulder.

1512
01:03:16,292 --> 01:03:17,418
After that, I couldn't punch.

1513
01:03:17,502 --> 01:03:19,462
I instinctively knew
she had done something,

1514
01:03:19,545 --> 01:03:21,339
so I confronted her after the match.

1515
01:03:21,422 --> 01:03:23,299
So, until then, you didn't know her?

1516
01:03:23,382 --> 01:03:24,509
I didn't.

1517
01:03:24,592 --> 01:03:29,180
- But...
- You're saying that someone

1518
01:03:29,263 --> 01:03:31,140
put Lee Gun-mi up to it.

1519
01:03:31,224 --> 01:03:33,559
{\an8}Gun-mi said someone
had ordered her to do it.

1520
01:03:33,643 --> 01:03:35,478
{\an8}So what next?
I told her to bring that punk to me.

1521
01:03:35,561 --> 01:03:39,607
{\an8}That means the person
who ordered Lee Gun-mi to do it

1522
01:03:39,690 --> 01:03:41,943
{\an8}was Lee Eighty,
as in the name on the business card.

1523
01:03:42,026 --> 01:03:44,111
{\an8}Because she hired a private investigator...

1524
01:03:44,195 --> 01:03:45,613
{\an8}Jang Private Investigator strikes again.

1525
01:03:45,696 --> 01:03:47,198
{\an8}- Jang Private Investigator?
- Right.

1526
01:03:47,281 --> 01:03:50,076
{\an8}She had Jang Private Investigator
look into Lee Eighty,

1527
01:03:50,159 --> 01:03:52,995
{\an8}and he discovered that Lee Eighty
frequented our neighborhood.

1528
01:03:53,079 --> 01:03:54,080
{\an8}- Lee Eighty?
- Yes.

1529
01:03:54,163 --> 01:03:58,960
{\an8}Do you really think I opened a shop
in this neighborhood just to earn money?

1530
01:03:59,043 --> 01:04:00,753
{\an8}I came here for Lee Eighty.

1531
01:04:00,837 --> 01:04:01,879
{\an8}But then,

1532
01:04:01,963 --> 01:04:03,923
{\an8}something unexpected happened.

1533
01:04:04,006 --> 01:04:05,258
{\an8}What now?

1534
01:04:05,341 --> 01:04:09,303
{\an8}You lost your temper again yesterday,
didn't you?

1535
01:04:09,387 --> 01:04:10,847
{\an8}- What happened?
- What is it?

1536
01:04:10,930 --> 01:04:13,933
{\an8}- She beat the crap out of someone.
- Oh dear.

1537
01:04:14,016 --> 01:04:15,560
{\an8}This is what happened.

1538
01:04:15,643 --> 01:04:18,145
{\an8}Shortly before the murder took place,

1539
01:04:18,229 --> 01:04:20,606
{\an8}Ms. Chicken and the victim
settled for 20 million won,

1540
01:04:20,690 --> 01:04:22,817
{\an8}when the victim was injured enough
to be hospitalized for eight weeks.

1541
01:04:22,900 --> 01:04:23,901
{\an8}- Oh my.
- Oh dear.

1542
01:04:23,985 --> 01:04:25,778
{\an8}He probably also thought
the amount was excessive,

1543
01:04:25,862 --> 01:04:27,655
{\an8}given that he wrote
"a whopping" 20 million won.

1544
01:04:27,738 --> 01:04:31,284
{\an8}I paid him well to get released quickly.
I didn't want the hassle.

1545
01:04:31,367 --> 01:04:33,411
- All right.
- Who did she assault?

1546
01:04:33,494 --> 01:04:36,205
The victim's name is Gu Ninety.

1547
01:04:36,289 --> 01:04:37,832
- Gu Ninety?
- She beat up Gu Ninety?

1548
01:04:37,915 --> 01:04:39,709
- Lee Eighty and now Gu Ninety?
- That's his name?

1549
01:04:39,792 --> 01:04:41,878
{\an8}- Do they belong to the Hair-Parting Gang?
- I suppose.

1550
01:04:41,961 --> 01:04:43,713
{\an8}So, that's not the same person
as Lee Eight.

1551
01:04:43,796 --> 01:04:45,381
{\an8}- It's Lee Eighty, not Lee Eight.
- Right, Eighty.

1552
01:04:45,464 --> 01:04:47,925
{\an8}- It's a completely different person.
- Lee Eight exists as well.

1553
01:04:48,009 --> 01:04:48,926
{\an8}Anyhow, Lee Eighty.

1554
01:04:49,010 --> 01:04:51,554
{\an8}Lee Eighty and Lee Eight
are completely different people.

1555
01:04:51,637 --> 01:04:52,930
{\an8}Isn't Lee Eighty Chinese?

1556
01:04:53,014 --> 01:04:55,057
{\an8}Fine, let me explain.

1557
01:04:55,141 --> 01:04:58,561
- Gun-mi said she had Lee Eighty with her.
- Okay.

1558
01:04:58,644 --> 01:05:00,313
- So I went and beat the crap out of him.
- Right.

1559
01:05:00,396 --> 01:05:01,981
But he turned out to be Gu Ninety!

1560
01:05:02,064 --> 01:05:04,609
- Right.
- His hair was parted more to the side.

1561
01:05:04,692 --> 01:05:07,236
- It was 90% to the side.
- Oh dear!

1562
01:05:07,320 --> 01:05:10,406
{\an8}But before I could do anything,
someone called the cops.

1563
01:05:10,489 --> 01:05:12,408
{\an8}What else could I have done
but settle quickly?

1564
01:05:12,491 --> 01:05:15,161
{\an8}There was no time to waste
if I wanted to catch Lee Eighty.

1565
01:05:15,244 --> 01:05:18,080
{\an8}Okay, moving on.
Did any of you stop by the police station?

1566
01:05:18,164 --> 01:05:19,040
{\an8}Not yet.

1567
01:05:19,123 --> 01:05:20,583
{\an8}At the police station,

1568
01:05:20,666 --> 01:05:22,627
I found a list of roadblocks

1569
01:05:23,210 --> 01:05:27,715
that were set up at specific times
because of the flood.

1570
01:05:28,591 --> 01:05:29,967
The list shows

1571
01:05:30,051 --> 01:05:31,469
that the road between Hansim Pocha

1572
01:05:31,552 --> 01:05:33,638
and Mr. Attorney's building
was sectioned off.

1573
01:05:33,721 --> 01:05:34,680
Yes, that's right.

1574
01:05:34,764 --> 01:05:37,058
Didn't they block that section off
due to flooding?

1575
01:05:37,141 --> 01:05:39,560
{\an8}The road was blocked once again
at 11:15 p.m.

1576
01:05:39,644 --> 01:05:42,146
Before that,
it was blocked until 10:30 p.m.

1577
01:05:42,229 --> 01:05:44,649
So there was a brief period
when it wasn't blocked.

1578
01:05:44,732 --> 01:05:47,234
{\an8}Oh, so there was a period
when traffic wasn't restricted?

1579
01:05:47,318 --> 01:05:48,486
{\an8}During that time...

1580
01:05:49,779 --> 01:05:51,989
{\an8}Detective, now that I think about it,

1581
01:05:52,073 --> 01:05:53,699
{\an8}if that road is blocked,

1582
01:05:53,783 --> 01:05:56,369
{\an8}we have to take a detour
past the police station.

1583
01:05:56,452 --> 01:06:01,457
{\an8}But there's always an officer
standing guard outside.

1584
01:06:01,540 --> 01:06:03,918
{\an8}If the culprit didn't want to be seen,

1585
01:06:04,001 --> 01:06:06,087
{\an8}they had to go through
the restricted area.

1586
01:06:06,170 --> 01:06:07,463
{\an8}Can't you also go around the back?

1587
01:06:07,546 --> 01:06:09,340
{\an8}That's something to think about.

1588
01:06:09,423 --> 01:06:10,925
{\an8}Anyway, that's all I have for now.

1589
01:06:11,008 --> 01:06:12,426
{\an8}CULPRIT'S ROUTE DEPENDS ON THE TIME
THE CRIME WAS COMMITTED

1590
01:06:12,510 --> 01:06:14,762
{\an8}Now, let's hear from Ms. Chicken.

1591
01:06:14,845 --> 01:06:16,555
{\an8}- Sure.
- Please go ahead.

1592
01:06:16,639 --> 01:06:19,183
{\an8}I have a few follow-up questions for her.

1593
01:06:19,266 --> 01:06:21,018
{\an8}Is using an animal's name
a family naming tradition?

1594
01:06:21,102 --> 01:06:22,186
{\an8}Shut it.

1595
01:06:23,562 --> 01:06:25,189
{\an8}WATCH YOUR MANNERS...

1596
01:06:25,272 --> 01:06:27,984
{\an8}Since C-store came at me, I'll bite back.

1597
01:06:28,067 --> 01:06:32,154
{\an8}I found texts between him and the victim
on the victim's phone.

1598
01:06:32,238 --> 01:06:33,114
{\an8}Really?

1599
01:06:33,197 --> 01:06:36,617
{\an8}At 9:05 p.m.,
C-store texted, "What's this?"

1600
01:06:36,701 --> 01:06:38,285
{\an8}To that, the victim replied,
"Hope you're ready."

1601
01:06:38,953 --> 01:06:39,996
{\an8}She knew something.

1602
01:06:40,079 --> 01:06:42,039
{\an8}I doubt it was about a nice surprise.

1603
01:06:42,123 --> 01:06:43,082
{\an8}That's exactly what it was.

1604
01:06:43,165 --> 01:06:45,793
{\an8}It was a surprise?
What was it about? Tell me.

1605
01:06:45,876 --> 01:06:47,128
{\an8}Don't stall and spill.

1606
01:06:47,211 --> 01:06:48,796
{\an8}What made you say, "What's this?"

1607
01:06:48,879 --> 01:06:50,881
{\an8}- A gift.
- What sort of gift?

1608
01:06:50,965 --> 01:06:53,050
{\an8}- Just a gift.
- What was in the gift?

1609
01:06:53,134 --> 01:06:53,968
{\an8}A gift.

1610
01:06:54,051 --> 01:06:56,220
{\an8}Okay, whatever. Just cut the crap.

1611
01:06:56,303 --> 01:06:59,890
Why did you call her at 9:57 p.m.?

1612
01:06:59,974 --> 01:07:02,018
- Hey! Why did you call her at 9:57 p.m.?
- Sorry?

1613
01:07:02,101 --> 01:07:03,394
Well, I was curious.

1614
01:07:03,477 --> 01:07:05,980
{\an8}- Did he call Gun-mi at 9:57 p.m.?
- C-store called her.

1615
01:07:06,063 --> 01:07:06,981
{\an8}No one picked up.

1616
01:07:07,064 --> 01:07:09,150
{\an8}Did you kill her for not picking up?

1617
01:07:09,233 --> 01:07:12,111
Come on. I don't kill someone
for not picking up my call.

1618
01:07:12,194 --> 01:07:13,029
I'm serious.

1619
01:07:13,112 --> 01:07:15,364
{\an8}Anyway, I checked out the house
that Gun-mi,

1620
01:07:15,448 --> 01:07:18,576
{\an8}or should I say Gun-jun, lived in.

1621
01:07:18,659 --> 01:07:21,537
{\an8}She got a notice
about her electricity being cut off.

1622
01:07:21,620 --> 01:07:24,165
{\an8}That, among other things,
suggested she was preparing to leave town.

1623
01:07:24,248 --> 01:07:27,543
{\an8}She had only rented the house
for about a month, anyway.

1624
01:07:27,626 --> 01:07:29,170
She told me she came to get her revenge,

1625
01:07:29,253 --> 01:07:33,132
so maybe she was leaving because
she'd already done what she came to do.

1626
01:07:33,215 --> 01:07:35,926
Didn't you see the plane ticket
she had in her wallet?

1627
01:07:36,010 --> 01:07:36,969
{\an8}- I saw it.
- There was one.

1628
01:07:37,053 --> 01:07:37,928
{\an8}She booked a flight

1629
01:07:38,012 --> 01:07:41,640
{\an8}- Bound for Russia.
- for 11:59 p.m. on the 12th.

1630
01:07:41,724 --> 01:07:42,850
{\an8}- Right.
- For 11:59 p.m.?

1631
01:07:42,933 --> 01:07:45,936
{\an8}I believe
this is somehow tied to the case,

1632
01:07:46,020 --> 01:07:47,897
{\an8}so we should look into it.

1633
01:07:48,481 --> 01:07:49,356
{\an8}Mister.

1634
01:07:50,024 --> 01:07:50,858
{\an8}Me?

1635
01:07:50,941 --> 01:07:52,943
{\an8}What did you do before running the club?

1636
01:07:53,569 --> 01:07:54,653
{\an8}Me?

1637
01:07:54,737 --> 01:07:56,030
{\an8}I used to work for a company.

1638
01:07:56,113 --> 01:07:57,406
{\an8}What kind of company?

1639
01:07:57,990 --> 01:07:59,617
{\an8}- I was a civil servant.
- A civil servant?

1640
01:08:00,201 --> 01:08:01,660
{\an8}He used to be a fuzz.

1641
01:08:02,244 --> 01:08:03,079
{\an8}What?

1642
01:08:03,162 --> 01:08:04,455
{\an8}Oh, come on.

1643
01:08:04,538 --> 01:08:06,624
{\an8}I was a detective, so what?
That's not a crime, is it?

1644
01:08:06,707 --> 01:08:09,502
{\an8}- He was a crooked cop.
- So you were getting inside information.

1645
01:08:09,585 --> 01:08:13,005
{\an8}I found an official apology
he wrote a while ago.

1646
01:08:13,089 --> 01:08:15,758
{\an8}He was on the narcotics teams
at the Gangdam Police Station.

1647
01:08:15,841 --> 01:08:17,051
Yeah, I handled narcotics cases.

1648
01:08:17,134 --> 01:08:19,970
He worked at Gangdam Police Station
and carried out coercive investigations.

1649
01:08:20,054 --> 01:08:22,890
- Even back then, he had quite the temper.
- Right.

1650
01:08:22,973 --> 01:08:25,059
Anyway, I found
an official apology he wrote.

1651
01:08:25,142 --> 01:08:27,478
{\an8}But do you know who had it?

1652
01:08:28,354 --> 01:08:30,856
{\an8}Lee Gun-jun. He had it.

1653
01:08:30,940 --> 01:08:31,857
{\an8}- What?
- What?

1654
01:08:31,941 --> 01:08:33,234
{\an8}Lee Gun-jun had it.

1655
01:08:33,317 --> 01:08:36,862
{\an8}How did he end up with a letter
you had written in 2000?

1656
01:08:36,946 --> 01:08:40,741
{\an8}I found one more suspicious item,
but I'm not sure if it's related.

1657
01:08:40,825 --> 01:08:43,119
{\an8}On Lee Gun-jun's desk at his place,

1658
01:08:43,202 --> 01:08:45,579
{\an8}there was a picture frame
with a photo ripped out of it.

1659
01:08:45,663 --> 01:08:48,415
{\an8}Only a ripped corner was left behind,

1660
01:08:48,499 --> 01:08:51,502
{\an8}and you could see
what resembled the police logo.

1661
01:08:51,585 --> 01:08:55,005
{\an8}So that got me thinking that maybe
Mr. Club was worried

1662
01:08:55,089 --> 01:08:57,341
{\an8}about being connected to Gun-jun
and took the photo with him.

1663
01:08:57,424 --> 01:08:59,135
{\an8}Were you ever in his room?

1664
01:08:59,218 --> 01:09:02,513
{\an8}- This is embarrassing to say.
- Go on.

1665
01:09:03,180 --> 01:09:05,891
{\an8}I met that girl

1666
01:09:05,975 --> 01:09:08,477
{\an8}- while having drinks.
- Okay.

1667
01:09:08,561 --> 01:09:11,480
{\an8}I wanted to start
a little something with her,

1668
01:09:12,314 --> 01:09:13,941
{\an8}but when I woke up the next morning,

1669
01:09:14,024 --> 01:09:15,693
{\an8}I noticed I had been robbed of everything

1670
01:09:15,776 --> 01:09:17,653
{\an8}- at the hotel.
- I see.

1671
01:09:17,736 --> 01:09:19,530
{\an8}- Unbelievable.
- And that tiny bitch had already left.

1672
01:09:19,613 --> 01:09:23,742
{\an8}I'm guessing that's when she took
my wallet and the written apology.

1673
01:09:23,826 --> 01:09:26,287
{\an8}- She took them all.
- And I guess she just kept them.

1674
01:09:26,370 --> 01:09:28,706
{\an8}Why were you at Lee Gun-mi's house?

1675
01:09:28,789 --> 01:09:31,333
{\an8}All I remembered about her was her name,

1676
01:09:31,417 --> 01:09:33,836
{\an8}but despite my best efforts,
I couldn't find her.

1677
01:09:33,919 --> 01:09:35,671
{\an8}But after several years,

1678
01:09:35,754 --> 01:09:39,383
{\an8}she showed up at my club with An Dancer.

1679
01:09:39,466 --> 01:09:41,886
{\an8}They were supposedly friends
and joined at the hip.

1680
01:09:41,969 --> 01:09:44,722
{\an8}I knew I recognized her,

1681
01:09:44,805 --> 01:09:47,141
{\an8}so I checked the sign-in sheet.

1682
01:09:47,224 --> 01:09:48,934
{\an8}And it really was Lee Gun-mi.

1683
01:09:49,018 --> 01:09:52,438
{\an8}Something felt off about her,

1684
01:09:52,521 --> 01:09:53,981
{\an8}so I went to her place.

1685
01:09:54,064 --> 01:09:56,108
{\an8}And what about the photo? What was it of?

1686
01:09:56,192 --> 01:09:57,568
{\an8}I went to her place

1687
01:09:57,651 --> 01:09:59,904
{\an8}and saw a family photo

1688
01:09:59,987 --> 01:10:01,530
{\an8}with someone I recognized.

1689
01:10:02,531 --> 01:10:05,284
{\an8}There was this colleague
from my days back in the force,

1690
01:10:05,367 --> 01:10:07,828
{\an8}and she had a photo of his family.

1691
01:10:07,912 --> 01:10:09,079
{\an8}What?

1692
01:10:09,163 --> 01:10:11,832
{\an8}- A former cop colleague?
- Was she the daughter

1693
01:10:11,916 --> 01:10:13,125
{\an8}of a former colleague?

1694
01:10:13,209 --> 01:10:14,418
{\an8}You mean, son.

1695
01:10:14,501 --> 01:10:17,379
{\an8}Was Lee Gun-mi in the photo?

1696
01:10:17,463 --> 01:10:21,300
{\an8}The photo was taken a long time ago,
so I couldn't be sure.

1697
01:10:21,383 --> 01:10:24,720
{\an8}So, there's a good chance
you worked with Lee Gun-jun's father.

1698
01:10:24,803 --> 01:10:26,805
{\an8}Okay, that'll be all.

1699
01:10:26,889 --> 01:10:29,683
{\an8}- Next, I'll move on to Ms. Dancer.
- Sure.

1700
01:10:29,767 --> 01:10:32,937
{\an8}Your Story Highlights had captions
like "Eternal friendship"

1701
01:10:33,020 --> 01:10:36,649
{\an8}and "Man or alien, I don't care."

1702
01:10:36,732 --> 01:10:38,400
{\an8}But I guess you had a falling out,

1703
01:10:38,484 --> 01:10:42,071
{\an8}because the photos you took on August 7th
had been ripped at some point.

1704
01:10:42,154 --> 01:10:43,781
{\an8}- Yeah, right down the middle.
- Exactly.

1705
01:10:43,864 --> 01:10:45,199
{\an8}That was the day

1706
01:10:45,282 --> 01:10:48,202
I saw Gun-mi
walk into Gold Spoon's office.

1707
01:10:49,578 --> 01:10:52,998
I had confided in her
about how I felt, you see.

1708
01:10:53,832 --> 01:10:56,377
I told her that I liked Gold Spoon a lot.

1709
01:10:56,460 --> 01:11:00,464
{\an8}Even though she knew that,
she went into his office.

1710
01:11:00,547 --> 01:11:02,424
{\an8}It's only normal
that it pissed me off, right?

1711
01:11:02,508 --> 01:11:03,384
{\an8}Right, of course.

1712
01:11:03,467 --> 01:11:06,095
{\an8}So, I said that was it for us
and ripped up the photo.

1713
01:11:06,178 --> 01:11:07,388
{\an8}Why did you tape it back together?

1714
01:11:07,471 --> 01:11:10,557
{\an8}- I was so mad, so I called her that night.
- Okay.

1715
01:11:10,641 --> 01:11:12,685
{\an8}That's when I learned the truth.

1716
01:11:12,768 --> 01:11:16,480
{\an8}She told me she was born a man
but had the heart of a woman.

1717
01:11:16,563 --> 01:11:18,482
{\an8}And because of that,
she could never be with Gold Spoon.

1718
01:11:18,565 --> 01:11:19,733
{\an8}At the time,

1719
01:11:19,817 --> 01:11:21,485
{\an8}I didn't know Mr. Attorney's passion

1720
01:11:21,568 --> 01:11:24,488
{\an8}for heart-shaped nostrils
trumped all else.

1721
01:11:24,571 --> 01:11:25,572
{\an8}- Hold on.
- Yes?

1722
01:11:25,656 --> 01:11:27,574
{\an8}When did you first meet Gun-mi?

1723
01:11:27,658 --> 01:11:28,867
{\an8}Gun-mi?

1724
01:11:28,951 --> 01:11:31,745
{\an8}She came up to me one day
when I was working at the club

1725
01:11:31,829 --> 01:11:33,789
{\an8}and said I was pretty.

1726
01:11:33,872 --> 01:11:35,165
{\an8}She was there as a guest?

1727
01:11:35,249 --> 01:11:38,043
{\an8}Yes. She told me
she was my number one fan.

1728
01:11:38,127 --> 01:11:41,046
{\an8}Once I learned we were the same age,
we became really close.

1729
01:11:41,130 --> 01:11:43,882
{\an8}I do believe that they patched things up

1730
01:11:43,966 --> 01:11:46,844
{\an8}because Lee Gun-mi texted her saying,

1731
01:11:46,927 --> 01:11:50,431
{\an8}"I'll be outside Slam
at 9:30 p.m. with the money."

1732
01:11:50,514 --> 01:11:53,017
{\an8}Dancer texted her multiple times,
but Gun-mi didn't respond

1733
01:11:53,100 --> 01:11:54,310
{\an8}even after 9:40 p.m.

1734
01:11:54,393 --> 01:11:57,354
{\an8}- Dancer couldn't reach her.
- So basically, no contact.

1735
01:11:57,438 --> 01:12:00,441
{\an8}- Was she going to give her the money?
- Detective, this is what you must note.

1736
01:12:00,524 --> 01:12:03,193
{\an8}The timeline of events
seems especially important today.

1737
01:12:03,277 --> 01:12:07,072
{\an8}Why didn't the victim
reply even after 9:40 p.m.?

1738
01:12:07,156 --> 01:12:09,325
{\an8}Mr. Gold Spoon said

1739
01:12:09,408 --> 01:12:13,412
{\an8}- he last saw Lee Gun-jun at 9:45 p.m.
- Yes, 9:45 p.m.

1740
01:12:13,495 --> 01:12:19,126
{\an8}C-store called the victim at 9:57 p.m.,
but the call was missed.

1741
01:12:19,209 --> 01:12:21,337
{\an8}Again, the victim didn't pick up.

1742
01:12:21,420 --> 01:12:24,256
{\an8}Please make note of that timeline.

1743
01:12:24,340 --> 01:12:25,883
{\an8}Got it.

1744
01:12:25,966 --> 01:12:28,260
{\an8}And here I thought
you were all fists and no brains.

1745
01:12:29,011 --> 01:12:29,970
{\an8}All right.

1746
01:12:30,054 --> 01:12:32,014
{\an8}FINAL BRIEFING: JANG DETECTIVE

1747
01:12:32,097 --> 01:12:33,891
{\an8}Detective, did you find much?

1748
01:12:34,516 --> 01:12:38,520
{\an8}The first question I have
is for An Dancer.

1749
01:12:39,438 --> 01:12:45,402
{\an8}How did you end up working at Club Slam?

1750
01:12:46,987 --> 01:12:47,821
Me?

1751
01:12:48,697 --> 01:12:50,949
You saw what I looked like before.

1752
01:12:52,034 --> 01:12:53,077
It was really bad.

1753
01:12:53,160 --> 01:12:57,873
After ten failed auditions,
Star-Star Entertainment took me in.

1754
01:12:57,956 --> 01:13:02,252
They said I could make it
with some minor tweaks to my appearance.

1755
01:13:02,336 --> 01:13:05,547
{\an8}That's how I ended up
getting a full body makeover.

1756
01:13:05,631 --> 01:13:07,174
{\an8}They had me lose weight as well.

1757
01:13:07,257 --> 01:13:10,427
{\an8}Since then, people have told me
I look like AN YUJIN of IVE.

1758
01:13:10,511 --> 01:13:11,887
{\an8}I get that pretty often.

1759
01:13:11,970 --> 01:13:15,516
{\an8}- No way. She's way prettier.
- You're like a half-baked version of her.

1760
01:13:15,599 --> 01:13:16,892
{\an8}Who? IU?

1761
01:13:16,975 --> 01:13:19,186
{\an8}- So? Is that when you got piles of debt?
- Anyway, moving on.

1762
01:13:19,269 --> 01:13:21,688
{\an8}- I got the 400-million-won debt then.
- Because of the full body makeover?

1763
01:13:21,772 --> 01:13:22,606
{\an8}It cost 400 million won?

1764
01:13:22,689 --> 01:13:25,234
{\an8}- Jeez, just what did you get done?
- I can't say I'm surprised by that number.

1765
01:13:25,317 --> 01:13:26,985
{\an8}Being reborn would've been quicker.

1766
01:13:27,069 --> 01:13:29,238
{\an8}- The process was absolute hell.
- Really?

1767
01:13:29,321 --> 01:13:31,073
{\an8}- They promised me a debut as an artist.
- Right.

1768
01:13:31,156 --> 01:13:33,409
{\an8}But I wasn't aware of my debt until then.

1769
01:13:33,492 --> 01:13:36,120
{\an8}When I thought my debut
was finally happening,

1770
01:13:36,203 --> 01:13:39,456
{\an8}they showed me their books
and said I couldn't debut

1771
01:13:39,540 --> 01:13:41,417
{\an8}until I cleared my 400-million-won debt.

1772
01:13:42,000 --> 01:13:44,753
{\an8}How could I have that kind of money
when I spent most of my life as a trainee?

1773
01:13:44,837 --> 01:13:47,589
{\an8}When I told them
I had no money to pay off my debt,

1774
01:13:47,673 --> 01:13:49,716
{\an8}they told me to work part-time.

1775
01:13:50,300 --> 01:13:53,345
{\an8}Have you heard of drop-offs?

1776
01:13:53,429 --> 01:13:55,472
{\an8}It's what people do these days.

1777
01:13:55,556 --> 01:13:56,432
{\an8}As in drug dealing?

1778
01:13:56,515 --> 01:13:58,434
{\an8}I'd drop off a package somewhere.

1779
01:13:58,517 --> 01:13:59,518
{\an8}- For someone to collect.
- Yes.

1780
01:13:59,601 --> 01:14:02,312
{\an8}- That's what they made me do.
- What has the world come to?

1781
01:14:02,396 --> 01:14:05,399
{\an8}Without 400 million won,
what option did I have? So, I did it.

1782
01:14:05,482 --> 01:14:08,694
{\an8}But then, the police showed up.

1783
01:14:09,403 --> 01:14:10,779
{\an8}THE POLICE?

1784
01:14:10,863 --> 01:14:11,905
{\an8}So I went with them.

1785
01:14:11,989 --> 01:14:15,617
{\an8}But instead of the police station,
they took me to a club.

1786
01:14:15,701 --> 01:14:18,745
{\an8}There, Mr. Kim gave me two options.

1787
01:14:18,829 --> 01:14:20,998
{\an8}Either I go to jail

1788
01:14:21,081 --> 01:14:24,251
{\an8}- or work at the club.
- What a scumbag.

1789
01:14:24,334 --> 01:14:27,546
{\an8}I chose to work at the club,
and 150 million won was added to my debt.

1790
01:14:27,629 --> 01:14:29,047
{\an8}That was the cost of staying out of jail.

1791
01:14:29,131 --> 01:14:29,965
{\an8}Hey!

1792
01:14:30,048 --> 01:14:32,885
{\an8}- That was stated in the contract.
- You got a good deal, Dancer.

1793
01:14:32,968 --> 01:14:34,136
{\an8}- You hear me?
- Thank you.

1794
01:14:34,219 --> 01:14:37,806
{\an8}- Look at your old photos and reflect.
- Okay.

1795
01:14:37,890 --> 01:14:41,268
{\an8}- That's how you ended up working at Slam.
- Yes, that's right.

1796
01:14:41,351 --> 01:14:44,146
{\an8}The pieces are finally coming together.

1797
01:14:44,229 --> 01:14:47,483
{\an8}At Mr. Club's office
inside the club he runs,

1798
01:14:47,566 --> 01:14:52,279
{\an8}I obtained a list of VIP parties.

1799
01:14:52,362 --> 01:14:56,825
{\an8}Our very own Mr. Gold Spoon
was also among the VIP guests.

1800
01:14:56,909 --> 01:14:59,161
{\an8}I attended parties
held at a club in a building I own.

1801
01:14:59,244 --> 01:15:01,413
{\an8}- Of course, he was there.
- What's wrong with that?

1802
01:15:01,497 --> 01:15:04,917
{\an8}Influential politicians, business leaders,
and other prominent figures attended.

1803
01:15:05,000 --> 01:15:08,545
{\an8}Everyone who's anyone shows up
at the VIP parties at my club.

1804
01:15:08,629 --> 01:15:11,757
{\an8}Everyone's bound to show up
on a list or two if you dig like this.

1805
01:15:11,840 --> 01:15:12,883
{\an8}Absolutely.

1806
01:15:12,966 --> 01:15:14,176
{\an8}- Anyway,
- I'm jealous.

1807
01:15:14,259 --> 01:15:16,428
{\an8}where is it that these people

1808
01:15:16,512 --> 01:15:19,640
{\an8}enjoy their VIP parties?

1809
01:15:19,723 --> 01:15:23,477
{\an8}I managed to find the very room
where the parties took place.

1810
01:15:23,560 --> 01:15:25,687
{\an8}- You did?
- How did you get in there?

1811
01:15:26,980 --> 01:15:28,023
{\an8}A key card?

1812
01:15:30,025 --> 01:15:33,111
{\an8}ONLY A SECRET CARD CAN UNLOCK THE DOOR

1813
01:15:35,322 --> 01:15:36,615
Unbelievable.

1814
01:15:38,325 --> 01:15:40,035
Are you kidding me?

1815
01:15:43,330 --> 01:15:45,040
So this is what you've been up to.

1816
01:15:46,375 --> 01:15:47,626
{\an8}Unbelievable.

1817
01:15:47,709 --> 01:15:50,003
{\an8}They were doing drugs at these parties.

1818
01:15:50,087 --> 01:15:51,797
{\an8}You've got to be kidding me.

1819
01:15:51,880 --> 01:15:54,258
{\an8}Cocaine, meth,

1820
01:15:54,341 --> 01:15:56,927
{\an8}- ecstasy, marijuana, and more.
- No, no.

1821
01:15:57,010 --> 01:16:00,180
{\an8}There were all sorts of drugs in there.

1822
01:16:00,264 --> 01:16:01,306
{\an8}Meat...

1823
01:16:01,390 --> 01:16:02,808
{\an8}MEAT = MARIJUANA, CANDY = ECSTASY

1824
01:16:02,891 --> 01:16:04,393
{\an8}- ...and others.
- Don't forget the candies.

1825
01:16:04,476 --> 01:16:05,727
{\an8}The menu referred to drugs, didn't it?

1826
01:16:05,811 --> 01:16:06,853
{\an8}ICE = METH

1827
01:16:06,937 --> 01:16:10,399
{\an8}Oh, so that's how it connects.
You bastard.

1828
01:16:10,482 --> 01:16:11,900
{\an8}I'm onto you now.

1829
01:16:11,984 --> 01:16:13,527
{\an8}You people are worse than animals.

1830
01:16:13,610 --> 01:16:15,028
{\an8}Mr. Gold Spoon.

1831
01:16:15,112 --> 01:16:16,947
{\an8}- I didn't do drugs.
- You're not a privileged rich man.

1832
01:16:17,030 --> 01:16:18,490
{\an8}- I didn't know.
- You're just an animal.

1833
01:16:18,574 --> 01:16:19,825
{\an8}I didn't know they were there.

1834
01:16:19,908 --> 01:16:22,286
{\an8}- I also have a question for An Dancer.
- Ask away.

1835
01:16:22,369 --> 01:16:23,245
{\an8}Were you aware

1836
01:16:23,328 --> 01:16:26,081
{\an8}of these so-called VIP parties?

1837
01:16:28,083 --> 01:16:29,626
{\an8}- I was aware of them, yes.
- You were?

1838
01:16:30,627 --> 01:16:33,797
{\an8}I know the adults are talking,
but can I add something to that?

1839
01:16:33,880 --> 01:16:35,090
{\an8}Sure, go ahead.

1840
01:16:35,173 --> 01:16:38,010
{\an8}He's scared he might get yelled at again.

1841
01:16:38,093 --> 01:16:41,346
{\an8}- Detective, let's go easy on the kid now.
- He's so timid now.

1842
01:16:41,430 --> 01:16:43,390
{\an8}- Let's go easy on the kid.
- Poor kid.

1843
01:16:43,473 --> 01:16:47,311
{\an8}You've finally developed something
called manners.

1844
01:16:47,394 --> 01:16:50,939
{\an8}Yes, it's definitely
made me more cautious.

1845
01:16:51,023 --> 01:16:52,816
{\an8}Earlier,

1846
01:16:52,899 --> 01:16:56,570
{\an8}Mr. Attorney mentioned
that he found Lee Eighty's business card.

1847
01:16:56,653 --> 01:16:58,447
{\an8}- Yes. "One billion, five hundred million."
-"One billion, five hundred million."

1848
01:16:58,530 --> 01:16:59,531
{\an8}Something about debuting in China too.

1849
01:16:59,615 --> 01:17:04,119
{\an8}That money could be what Dancer would earn
by debuting in China,

1850
01:17:04,202 --> 01:17:07,414
{\an8}but it might also be connected
to that tiny camera.

1851
01:17:07,497 --> 01:17:11,543
{\an8}She might've recorded
the VIP guests partying

1852
01:17:11,627 --> 01:17:14,880
{\an8}and traded the footage to settle her debt.

1853
01:17:15,464 --> 01:17:16,340
{\an8}Hey, An Dancer.

1854
01:17:16,423 --> 01:17:19,468
{\an8}Is that what you've been cooking
at my club?

1855
01:17:19,551 --> 01:17:21,303
{\an8}- Just spill it.
- Were you going to rat us out?

1856
01:17:21,386 --> 01:17:22,387
{\an8}First of all,

1857
01:17:23,472 --> 01:17:25,766
{\an8}- I have a debt of 550 million won.
- Right.

1858
01:17:25,849 --> 01:17:28,644
{\an8}Over the years,
I've only been able to pay off 50 million.

1859
01:17:28,727 --> 01:17:31,229
{\an8}Yeah, 50 million won
over the course of 5 years.

1860
01:17:31,313 --> 01:17:32,481
{\an8}That took me five years.

1861
01:17:32,564 --> 01:17:34,524
- At this rate--
- You only have 50 more years to go.

1862
01:17:34,608 --> 01:17:36,151
I confided in Gun-mi

1863
01:17:36,234 --> 01:17:37,778
{\an8}about my financial problems,

1864
01:17:37,861 --> 01:17:40,405
{\an8}and she told me what I could do.

1865
01:17:40,489 --> 01:17:45,035
{\an8}Selling photos of VIP guests doing drugs
to people in China

1866
01:17:45,118 --> 01:17:47,079
{\an8}could get me 500 million won per person.

1867
01:17:48,205 --> 01:17:50,207
{\an8}Is Lee Eighty the name
of an entertainment agency in China?

1868
01:17:50,290 --> 01:17:52,334
{\an8}No, Lee Eighty is a broker.

1869
01:17:52,417 --> 01:17:55,045
{\an8}I had to hand him the photos to get paid.

1870
01:17:55,128 --> 01:17:57,798
{\an8}Gun-mi also said she had found
a Chinese agency for me.

1871
01:17:57,881 --> 01:18:01,968
{\an8}Dropping off drugs in the VIP room
was part of my job anyway,

1872
01:18:02,552 --> 01:18:05,889
{\an8}so I made a hole in my purse like so.

1873
01:18:05,972 --> 01:18:10,102
{\an8}- For the camera?
- I hid it here to take photos.

1874
01:18:10,185 --> 01:18:12,396
{\an8}Doesn't Ms. Chicken also know Lee Eighty?

1875
01:18:12,479 --> 01:18:15,023
{\an8}- No, she's been hunting him down.
- She's looking for him.

1876
01:18:15,107 --> 01:18:19,986
{\an8}These two had been waiting for Lee Gun-mi
to find him and deliver him.

1877
01:18:20,696 --> 01:18:21,988
{\an8}These two are connected to Lee Eighty.

1878
01:18:22,072 --> 01:18:23,115
{\an8}- All right.
- We should try to find him.

1879
01:18:23,198 --> 01:18:24,032
{\an8}Sounds good.

1880
01:18:24,116 --> 01:18:26,743
{\an8}I have one last thing to ask.

1881
01:18:26,827 --> 01:18:28,120
{\an8}- C-store.
- Yes?

1882
01:18:28,203 --> 01:18:31,623
{\an8}You arrived at the meeting
for business owners at 10:10 p.m.

1883
01:18:31,707 --> 01:18:33,166
{\an8}- I showed up late.
- At 10:10 p.m.

1884
01:18:33,250 --> 01:18:36,044
{\an8}All attendees

1885
01:18:36,128 --> 01:18:39,339
{\an8}had to sign out when leaving the meeting.

1886
01:18:39,423 --> 01:18:42,259
{\an8}- That's right.
- Mr. Club signed out,

1887
01:18:42,342 --> 01:18:43,635
{\an8}as did Dancer.

1888
01:18:43,719 --> 01:18:45,846
{\an8}- She came as a proxy.
- Though she was representing someone else.

1889
01:18:45,929 --> 01:18:49,224
{\an8}You had to sign the form
to receive the support fund.

1890
01:18:49,307 --> 01:18:51,226
{\an8}Exactly. However,

1891
01:18:51,309 --> 01:18:56,356
{\an8}not only did you show up late,
but you left without signing out,

1892
01:18:56,440 --> 01:19:00,694
{\an8}even though
you could've received a support fund

1893
01:19:00,777 --> 01:19:03,196
{\an8}for your father-in-law's
convenience store.

1894
01:19:03,280 --> 01:19:05,449
{\an8}- His father-in-law...
- Why didn't he sign out?

1895
01:19:05,532 --> 01:19:07,784
{\an8}Why didn't you sign out when leaving?

1896
01:19:07,868 --> 01:19:11,121
{\an8}- He was furious when he arrived.
- Why were you so angry?

1897
01:19:11,204 --> 01:19:12,581
{\an8}Here's the thing.

1898
01:19:12,664 --> 01:19:14,207
{\an8}You said I was angry when I arrived.

1899
01:19:14,291 --> 01:19:15,834
{\an8}- Yeah, while you were on the phone.
- I was on the phone.

1900
01:19:15,917 --> 01:19:17,753
{\an8}I was on the phone

1901
01:19:17,836 --> 01:19:19,421
{\an8}with our delivery guy.

1902
01:19:19,504 --> 01:19:20,464
{\an8}Why were you angry at him?

1903
01:19:20,547 --> 01:19:23,759
{\an8}He couldn't park his truck
in front of the convenience store

1904
01:19:23,842 --> 01:19:26,470
{\an8}- because of the roadblocks.
- Right, they blocked the roads.

1905
01:19:26,553 --> 01:19:28,972
{\an8}I suggested he park in a different spot,

1906
01:19:29,055 --> 01:19:30,807
{\an8}but he said,

1907
01:19:30,891 --> 01:19:34,186
{\an8}"If I do, the truck will block
the view of the CCTV."

1908
01:19:34,936 --> 01:19:37,898
You mean the CCTV
in front of Sensibility Bar?

1909
01:19:37,981 --> 01:19:39,232
{\an8}Did he park there?

1910
01:19:39,316 --> 01:19:40,400
{\an8}He probably did

1911
01:19:40,484 --> 01:19:41,818
{\an8}and blocked the camera's view.

1912
01:19:41,902 --> 01:19:44,780
{\an8}Detective, his statement just now
shouldn't be taken lightly.

1913
01:19:44,863 --> 01:19:48,700
{\an8}Knowing when the CCTV
had been blocked seems crucial.

1914
01:19:48,784 --> 01:19:49,618
{\an8}She's right.

1915
01:19:49,701 --> 01:19:51,411
{\an8}- The camera outside Sensibility Bar.
- The truck, huh?

1916
01:19:51,495 --> 01:19:53,955
{\an8}New evidence has surfaced
regarding the delivery truck.

1917
01:19:54,039 --> 01:19:56,458
{\an8}- All right.
- The delivery truck.

1918
01:19:56,541 --> 01:19:57,793
{\an8}THE DETECTIVE'S 1ST ROUND OF VOTING

1919
01:19:57,876 --> 01:19:59,419
{\an8}I will cast my valuable vote.

1920
01:20:00,212 --> 01:20:01,171
{\an8}My vote...

1921
01:20:02,881 --> 01:20:04,424
{\an8}will go to the person
with a strong motive.

1922
01:20:04,508 --> 01:20:06,927
{\an8}THE DETECTIVE CASTS 2 VOTES TOTAL,
1 OF WHICH IS CAST AFTER THE BRIEFING

1923
01:20:07,010 --> 01:20:07,969
{\an8}Make a wise choice.

1924
01:20:08,053 --> 01:20:09,471
{\an8}So that's where it was.

1925
01:20:10,555 --> 01:20:15,018
{\an8}Detective, you'd better get this right
if you want to keep your badge.

1926
01:20:15,101 --> 01:20:17,854
{\an8}IN MEMORY OF A CERTAIN DETECTIVE
WITH SWAGGER WHO WAS BANNED FROM THE FORCE

1927
01:20:19,689 --> 01:20:20,857
{\an8}WHO WILL THE DETECTIVE VOTE FOR?

1928
01:20:20,941 --> 01:20:23,944
{\an8}There's something this one person has
that the other four don't.

1929
01:20:24,694 --> 01:20:25,654
{\an8}I'll tell you what that is.

1930
01:20:26,196 --> 01:20:29,491
{\an8}WHO DOES JANG DETECTIVE
CURRENTLY THINK THE CULPRIT IS?

1931
01:20:30,158 --> 01:20:31,660
{\an8}You have a family.

1932
01:20:33,703 --> 01:20:34,621
What does he mean?

1933
01:20:35,455 --> 01:20:36,289
A child.

1934
01:20:36,373 --> 01:20:37,958
You have a family.

1935
01:20:38,667 --> 01:20:40,001
{\an8}JANG DETECTIVE CHOSE JANG C-STORE

1936
01:20:40,085 --> 01:20:41,086
{\an8}What does he mean?

1937
01:20:41,878 --> 01:20:43,338
{\an8}- A child.
- What?

1938
01:20:43,421 --> 01:20:44,673
{\an8}You have a family of your own.

1939
01:20:44,756 --> 01:20:46,299
{\an8}For goodness' sake.

1940
01:20:47,175 --> 01:20:48,385
{\an8}I guess he has a point.

1941
01:20:48,468 --> 01:20:50,470
{\an8}The youngest person here
has a wife and a child.

1942
01:20:50,554 --> 01:20:52,138
{\an8}He even has a father-in-law.

1943
01:20:52,222 --> 01:20:54,850
{\an8}This is why I can't stand old-timers.

1944
01:20:54,933 --> 01:20:57,018
{\an8}Yet, you sounded like
a geezer yourself just now.

1945
01:20:57,102 --> 01:20:59,688
{\an8}Anyway, let's all get back out there,

1946
01:20:59,771 --> 01:21:02,065
{\an8}look for clues we missed,

1947
01:21:02,148 --> 01:21:04,025
{\an8}and catch the killer.

1948
01:21:04,109 --> 01:21:05,443
{\an8}Do better, damn it!

1949
01:21:08,071 --> 01:21:10,740
Reverting back
to your juvie days, are you?

1950
01:21:11,324 --> 01:21:12,284
That was intense.

1951
01:21:13,326 --> 01:21:14,369
Keep up the good work.

1952
01:21:14,452 --> 01:21:16,663
THE SCENE OF THE CRIME
WILL REVEAL THE CULPRIT

1953
01:21:16,746 --> 01:21:17,956
- Hello.
- Hello.

1954
01:21:18,039 --> 01:21:19,332
- Hello.
- How are you?

1955
01:21:19,416 --> 01:21:21,251
I bet he witnessed a lot of things.

1956
01:21:22,294 --> 01:21:24,296
Where should we even begin?

1957
01:21:24,379 --> 01:21:25,797
First of all,

1958
01:21:26,840 --> 01:21:29,259
I'd like to see what Mr. Club took.

1959
01:21:29,342 --> 01:21:31,094
- Oh, right.
- The photo.

1960
01:21:31,177 --> 01:21:32,888
{\an8}- Show us the photo.
- I'd like to see the photo.

1961
01:21:32,971 --> 01:21:35,473
{\an8}Mister, show us the photo
if you want to clear your name.

1962
01:21:35,557 --> 01:21:36,933
{\an8}Tell us where you put it.

1963
01:21:37,017 --> 01:21:38,435
{\an8}It's right here.

1964
01:21:38,518 --> 01:21:39,811
{\an8}- You have it with you?
- Pull it out.

1965
01:21:39,895 --> 01:21:41,396
{\an8}- I'll take it out.
- All right.

1966
01:21:42,147 --> 01:21:43,899
{\an8}- This is the photo.
- The family photo.

1967
01:21:44,482 --> 01:21:45,317
{\an8}Who's that?

1968
01:21:46,776 --> 01:21:47,652
{\an8}Who's that?

1969
01:21:49,988 --> 01:21:51,615
{\an8}It's Park Hyeon-jun!

1970
01:21:51,698 --> 01:21:52,824
{\an8}Hang on a second.

1971
01:21:52,908 --> 01:21:54,326
{\an8}That's Park Hyeon-jun.

1972
01:21:54,409 --> 01:21:55,994
{\an8}- Park Hyeon-jun?
- Park Hyeon-jun?

1973
01:21:56,077 --> 01:21:58,622
{\an8}- I thought his name was Lee Gun-jun.
- That's odd.

1974
01:21:58,705 --> 01:22:00,582
{\an8}It doesn't make any sense.

1975
01:22:00,665 --> 01:22:02,834
{\an8}How can he be Park Hyeon-jun?

1976
01:22:02,918 --> 01:22:04,002
{\an8}Then what about him?

1977
01:22:04,085 --> 01:22:05,879
{\an8}Park Hyeon-jun was a guy.

1978
01:22:05,962 --> 01:22:06,796
{\an8}Yeah, a guy.

1979
01:22:06,880 --> 01:22:09,299
{\an8}- Wasn't that Lee Gun-jun's alias?
- Wasn't he also Lee Gun-jun?

1980
01:23:11,528 --> 01:23:16,533
{\an8}Subtitle translation by: Yenny Kang

